const WRITEUP_SECTIONS = [{"title":"2025 Season Preview","year":"2025","html":"<p>MSUFFL 2025 Season Preview:</p>\n<br>\n<p>It\u2019s the last stress free weekend for the rest of the year. Enjoy a walk with your spouse, play with your children, finish up the show your binging, because starting later this week you will be agonizing over each of your teams decisions for the next four months.</p>\n<br>\n<p>Should I start a safe flex or go for upside? Is the guy who went off last week startable this week? Should I drop the 16th player on my roster who hasn\u2019t done anything for this guy on the waiver wire who also hasn\u2019t done anything, but might do something? Is this questionable guy going to play or should I start the guy playing on Friday (yes there are Friday games now) instead?</p>\n<br>\n<p>For now let\u2019s look at where we stand before the chaos of the NFL season ensues.</p>\n<br>\n<h3 class=\"wu-h3\">When do the playoffs start:</h3>\n<br>\n<p><strong>There\u2019s a YOU Sheriff in Town</strong> - the reigning first loser returns his entire squad including a top 3 QB, RB, and 3 top 10 WRs. He has the best starting lineup to start the season, adding David Montgomery in the draft, with decent depth acquired for cheap in Javonte Williams, Deebo Samuel, and Michael Pittman. Expect <em>Willen</em> to contend for a championship again this year as the early favorite.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Show me Your TDs</strong> - after a disappointing 2024 and a terrible draft, <em>Geraldo</em> decided to mortgage the future to get his team to look amazing on paper. He added two top 5 WRs, Justin Jefferson and A.J. Brown, to take a solid starting lineup to one of the best in the league. The move also created strong depth with George Pickens as his 5th WR after dealing DK Metcalf for a future first. If Jayden Daniels continues his previous season and Jonathan Taylor and De\u2019Von Achane stay healthy, anything short of a championship is a disappointment.</p>\n<br>\n<h3 class=\"wu-h3\">Strong playoff contenders:</h3>\n<br>\n<p><strong>The Benchwarmers</strong> - after riding Barkley to the championship in 2024, <em>Smith</em> decided to try and time the market by trading him away. He moved Barkley and the 12 pick for the 3rd pick, which netted him TreVeyon Henderson and allowed him to repurpose $97 in the draft. <em>Smith </em>turned his savings into Kenneth Walker and Tony Pollard. His roster is solid with good depth, but it would have been stronger with Barkley. He has a decent chance to defend his championship entering the season.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Windy City Waterboys</strong> - <em>Gillespie</em> is returning his full squad that made him a playoff team last year, so he didn\u2019t have many roster spots heading into the draft. Garrett Wilson and Dak Prescott were his big acquisitions who may or may not make an impact for him this season. While he has a solid starting lineup his depth is a little shaky and this lineup has yet to win a championship. Maybe this is the year!</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Can\u2019t Reed Can\u2019t Write</strong> - his team remains competitive as <em>Keaton </em>he kept his core of Josh Allen, Ceedee Lamb, Kyren Williams, and Terry McLaurin. <em>Keaton</em> was able to retain Isiah Pacheco, but that left him with few resources to fill out his roster. While the starting lineup looks weaker than his championship squad two season ago, he did a great job with low dollar players creating depth and a few potential starting options. Likely a playoff team, possibly a deep run.</p>\n<br>\n<p>Note: a team name change is in order\u2026 there has to be something he can do with Lamb, Coker, or Chubb.</p>\n<br>\n<h3 class=\"wu-h3\">Playoff hopefuls****:</h3>\n<br>\n<p><strong>The Nixon Administration</strong> - don\u2019t look now, but <em>Frink</em>\u2019s has some strong RB starters. Travis Etienne isn\u2019t the best third option, but he\u2019s a year removed from a strong season. <em>Frink</em> is deep at WR, especially after retaining Jameson Williams and pickup up DJ Moore, but doesn\u2019t have a true difference maker at the position. Despite that his starting lineup is good, even if his weekly starter decisions is going to be a constant struggle. I see the potential here but there are questions if Bo Nix is that good and if Mark Andrews is still a no doubt starter. <em>Frink</em> has solid squad, but given his history it was tough to move him up to the next tier.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Benches Don\u2019t Score Points I Do</strong> - <em>Butler</em> can go ahead and keep his team name considering his starting lineup strong, but he once again has no bench. He was able to retain two of his RFAs in James Cook and Breece Hall, securing two of the best RBs in the draft to go along with his Bengals Stack, Ja\u2019Marr Chase and Joe Burrow. He added Evan Engram for cheap and Cooper Kupp and Christian Kirk hoping to get some starters. However, <em>Butler </em>quickly realized he needed to make a move for another trustable starter and traded a future first for DK Metcalf. This team has potential to make the playoffs again, but can it bring <em>Butler</em> his first ever playoff win? Time will tell.</p>\n<br>\n<br>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Let\u2019s Cook</strong> - another team that kept most of their team from last season, only in this case, <em>Banchiu</em> and <em>Steinmetz</em> failed to make the playoffs last year. Their team isn\u2019t bad on paper, but they\u2019re relying on some older players - Derrick Henry, James Connor, Mike Evan - to keep extending their primes another year. They added some youth with their three draft picks, but none of them appear ready to make immediate impacts. <em>Banchiu</em> and <em>Steinmetz</em> are going to have to either make a move to get more depth or tank if one of their starters gets hurt. A lot of questions remain, but the talent is there entering the season.</p>\n<br>\n<h3 class=\"wu-h3\">Need to get lucky:</h3>\n<br>\n<p><strong>I\u2019ve Fallen and I Can\u2019t Get Up</strong> - <em>White</em> nailed the team name, he has an old and injury prone team. His team 3 years ago wouldn\u2019t even be fair\u2026 CMC, Alvin Kamara, Davante Adams, Adam Thielen, Hollywood Brown, and Darren Waller may all be retired this time next year (Waller for the second time). However you can see how this might work. As long as everyone is still healthy they can be solid, but they\u2019re likely past their prime. <em>White</em> has a few young, fun players in Isaac Teslaa and JCM/Bill, but neither are likely to be reliable fantasy players this season. The start of his season should be good, but he\u2019ll need health luck to keep the train going.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>The Mixon Administration</strong> - <em>Laethem</em> made a splash move before the draft really got going by sending his 3rd pick for the right to pay Barkley. This left him with little money to work with and somehow he ended up bringing Joe Mixon back on a team friendly deal. Mixon has since gone on IR creating a void at RB. This might be an opportunity for a name change given we already have \u201cThe Nixon Administration\u201d\u2026 Either Jerome Ford or Tank Bigsby will have to hit to keep <em>Laethem</em> competitive this season early on. He also needs a couple of his young receivers - Xavier Worthy, Ricky Pearsall, Khalil Shakir -  along with Caleb Williams to make a leap to truly compete.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Drunkin\u2019 Polacks</strong> - <em>Chorazcheski </em>had a great draft, getting Ashton Jeanty and Travis Hunter, two great talents, and adding Drake London, Aaron Jones, and Jaylen Waddle. He rebuilt his team completely, but unfortunately he had nothing to build up from. Brian Robinson, who was supposed to be a foundational piece, ended up getting run out of Washington, and despite three first round picks in the legendary 2024 draft he only netted one starter. Rashee Rice will also miss the first 6 games so he will need to survive that period and he could push for a playoff spot. The young team creates some variance, so if a couple of his bench guys start hot watch out!</p>\n<br>\n<h3 class=\"wu-h3\">Maybe by 2032:</h3>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Half Chubb</strong> - <em>Costello</em>\u2019s team looked competitive for about 30 minutes after the draft. He had a great draft with Omarion Hampton and RJ Harvey looking like strong rookie selections and Justin Jefferson and AJ Brown as elite starters. However, he was \u201cunder the influence\u201d and decided to go back into the tank by trading Jefferson and Brown for six, yes SIX, first round picks. We won\u2019t know who won the trade until, at the earliest, the end of this year, but more realistically it\u2019ll be a couple years. Some are saying <em>Costello</em> is scared to compete, others are questioning his competence, maybe he\u2019s playing chess and we\u2019re all playing fantasy football.</p>\n<br>\n<p class=\"wu-bold\">______________________________</p>\n<br>\n<p>Top draft day blunders:</p>\n<br>\n<p>It\u2019s the most exciting time of the year! Everyone, even <em>Butler</em> and <em>Frink</em> (the two owners who have yet to win a playoff game), have the same hope to win a championship as the league legends. Through the years owners have let the excitement, or maybe the booze, get to them, leading to some of the most memorable draft day blunders. Let\u2019s go back in time and enjoy some of those devastating moments.</p>\n<br>\n<p>Starting with our current champion <em>Smith</em> going big on JK Dobbins in 2023 for $50, more than Jonathan Taylor and Ceedee lamb, for Dobbins to go out and play less than 1 whole game. Some might say this is hindsight, but it was an obvious overpay at the time and turned out worse than anticipated. Big miss, but <em>Smith</em> managed to turn around and tank that season to secure the assets that ultimately netted him the 2024 championship.</p>\n<br>\n<p>Next up is <em>Geraldo</em>, going all-in on Nick Chubb for $130, which still remains the highest bid for a player. Chubb had a good year, but this was still a ridiculous sum for one player.  This is a record that is unlikely to be broken, but who knows\u2026this league is wildly unpredictable and there is always some draft day drama.</p>\n<br>\n<p><em>Banchiu</em> and <em>Steinmetz</em> have been the perfect pairing, like peanut butter and jelly or pepperoni and gumballs. However it hasn\u2019t always been sunshine and rainbows. As their first year as comanagers <em>Banchiu </em>and <em>Steinmetz</em> were on the phones in south haven wheeling and dealing talking themselves out of the young electric back, CMC, in favor of the freshly unretired, Marshawn Lynch, as their first pick of the new dynasty era of MSUFFL. While Marshawn tried to keep the dream alive, CMC went on to have a legendary fantasy</p>\n<br>\n<p>Everyone remembers <em>Frink</em> taking CEH over JT in 2020, leading to <em>White</em> winning two of the next 3 titles. He has lost more sleep over that one draft pick than any single decision he\u2019s ever made in his life. But people forget <em>Frink</em> could have probably won all 3 championships if he took JT and Justin Jefferson, instead of Henry Ruggs, with his other pick. Let\u2019s hope he learned his lesson this time around with multiple first rounders again</p>\n<br>\n<p>And finally we have the most memorable meltdown in 2014. His brain was waffled from the night before, in enemy territory without any preparation and failing to bring his laptop. <em>Butler</em> was on the clock with everyone pressuring him to make his pick and uh uh uh Legarrette Blount came out of his mouth before he could filter it out. While we have had many bad picks this one continues to be the one that comes to mind the quickest.</p>\n<br>\n<p>Everyone in the league continues to get more sharp and take their draft day with more focus than the ACT, so the expectation is there will be fewer blunders. However, when the booze starts flowing and the bright lights are on mistakes may still happen. Good luck to everyone and may you make all the right decisions to bring you joy for the next 5 months of your life!</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">A couple legendary draft memories</h2>\n<br>\n<li>Black-out Sunday, Nashville</li>\n<br>\n<li>Laethem Smirnoff Ice Chug, Put-in-Bay</li>\n<br>\n<li>Typhoon in Torch, Torch Lake</li>\n<br>\n<p class=\"wu-bold\">______________________________</p>\n<br>\n<p>**</p>"},{"title":"2024 Wrapped","year":"2024","html":"<p>MSUFFL 2024 Wrapped**</p>\n<br>\n<p>Another amazing season in the books. We finally saw <em>Butler</em> make the playoffs for the first time EVER and even led the league in scoring. <em>Willen</em> fumbled the bag due to his Bears homerism, by trusting Moore over Nabers in a must win situation. <em>Smith</em> made it back to the promise land after executing a perfect tank job (it doesn\u2019t hurt hitting big on Barkley either) giving <em>Chorazyczewski</em> some hope for next year. <em>Geraldo</em> missed the playoffs for just the second time in league history. <em>Costello</em> went from contender to rebuild before the season started, while <em>White</em> went from rebuild to contender to broken down throughout the season. <em>Frink</em> finally found some running backs! <em>Steinmetz</em> and <em>Banchiu</em> traded themselves into and out of the playoffs. <em>Laethem</em> actually made some trades this year. <em>Keaton</em> failed to defend his championship, while a championship continues to elude <em>Gillespie</em>. Better luck next year!</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Best 2024 Keeper Value:</strong> De\u2019Von Achane (<em>Geraldo</em>), Chase Brown (<em>Smith</em>), and Chuba Hubbard (<em>Frink</em>) - It was tough to choose just one, but the theme for this year\u2019s best keeper value is cheap, non-rookie deal, RBs for which we had 3 that finished as RB1s. All provided their team with a competitive edge and likely will into the foreseeable future.</p>\n<br>\n<p>Honorable Mention: Derrick Henry and Alvin Kamara (<em>Steinmetz</em>/<em>Banchiu</em>)</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Best Auction Value: </strong>JK Dobbins (<em>Smith</em>) - after going all in on Dobbins last year, <em>Smith</em> stayed committed to him again despite a seemingly catastrophic injury last year. While healthy, Dobbins was a reliable RB2 and only cost <em>Smith</em> $9 (compared to $50 last year). It was close between him and Kittle who <em>Lathem</em> got for a steal at $14 and gave him a competitive advantage at TE for the whole season.</p>\n<br>\n<p>Honorable Mentions: George Kittle (<em>Laethem</em>), Tee Higgins (<em>Gillespie</em>), Tony Pollard (Geraldo), Courtland Sutton (<em>Costello</em>)</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Best Rookie Draft Pick:</strong> Brian Thomas Jr. (<em>Smith</em>) - despite being the 5th WR off the board (and the 3rd picked by <em>Smith</em>), Thomas was strong from the start and dominated to finish the season off. He was a key piece in <em>Smith</em>\u2019s playoff run and should be the key to future success for <em>Smith.</em> Bucky Irving should get some love here too because rarely does one find a starting RB late in the second round.</p>\n<br>\n<p>Honorable Mentions: Bucky Irving (<em>Frink</em>), Brock Bowers (<em>Chorazyczewski</em>), Jayden Daniels and Ladd McConkey (<em>Geraldo</em>)</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Best Free Agent Pickup:</strong> Tyrone Tracy (<em>Costello</em>) - Tracy was putting up strong numbers in a bad offense, and while he didn\u2019t help <em>Costello</em> win games this season he will likely be a franchise piece going forward. Tracy might be in the Best Keeper Value for 2025 if the Giants can ever get their shit together. It\u2019s worth mentioning that <em>White</em> picked up two vets in Hunt and Smith to help him outkick his expected season finish.</p>\n<br>\n<p>Honorable Mentions: Kareem Hunt and Jonnu Smith (<em>White</em>), Bo Nix and Jerry Jeudy (<em>Frink</em>), Cade Otton (<em>Steinmetz</em>/<em>Banchiu</em>) - we\u2019ll always have those 3 weeks</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Best Trade: </strong>Justin Jefferson and James Cook for DJ Moore and Jeff\u2019s 2024 First Round Pick (#2, Malik Nabers) - There were a lot of really awesome trades this season and for the most part they were logical. This trade set up the major storylines for the season with <em>Butler</em> going ALL IN for the playoffs, while also setting <em>Willen</em> up for a deep playoff run only to choose Moore over Nabers to lose the \u2018ship.</p>\n<br>\n<p>Honorable Mentions: Ja\u2019Marr Chase for Jeff\u2019s 2025 and 2026 First Round picks (<em>Costello</em>), DeAndre Swift for Bobby\u2019s 2025 Second Round and 2026 First Round picks (<em>Chorazyczewski</em>), Alvin Kamara and Jared Goff for Jordan Love and Brad\u2019s 2025 First Round pick (<em>White</em>), Jaxon Smith-Njigba for DK Metcalf (<em>Steinmetz</em> and <em>Banchiu</em>)</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Worst Keeper Value:</strong> Christian McCaffery (<em>Costello</em>) - At the time it was not a bad decision (he was the best keeper value last season and appeared to be affordable still), <em>Costello</em> held on to CMC through injury for $74 and basically tanked his season. <em>Costello</em> was able to offload him to <em>White</em> to get his 2025 draft pick back so it wasn\u2019t a complete crusher, but it definitely set his franchise back a year at least.</p>\n<br>\n<p>Honorable Mention: Dak Prescott (<em>Willen</em>)</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Worst Auction Value:</strong> Travis Etienne (<em>Willen</em>) - Despite a strong start to his career, Etienne was not worth top tier RB value and was placed into a timeshare with a guy named Tank. <em>Willen</em> made sure to keep him at $61, which he could have spent elsewhere to get more depth. Overall it didn\u2019t kill <em>Willen</em>, but questions about his decision making are still out there because of this move and many more.</p>\n<br>\n<p>Honorable Mention: Javonte Williams (<em>Laethem</em>), Zamir White (<em>Frink</em>), Deebo Samuel (<em>Smith</em>), Travis Kelce (<em>Chorazyczewski</em>)</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Worst Rookie Draft Pick: </strong>Caleb Williams (<em>Laethem</em>) - his rookie season was terrible especially considering the hype he came in with and to add insult to injury Jayden Daniels has been awesome both as a real life and fantasy football QB. While Brooks was probably a worse pick due to injury and opportunity cost, Williams is looking like a bad pick due to on-field performance.</p>\n<br>\n<p>Honorable Mention: Jonathan Brooks (<em>Chorazyczewski</em>), Marvin Harrison Jr., Rome Odunze, and Blake Corum (<em>Smith</em>)</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Worst Trade: </strong>Bobby\u2019s 2026 Second Round and 2027 First Round picks for Devin Singletary and Jake Ferguson (<em>Willen</em>) - There were not a ton of bad trades this year as the motivations were clear for most teams. This one stands out as the only terrible trade since it didn\u2019t help <em>Willen</em> in any way and he traded a future first round pick for it. Don\u2019t drink and trade folks!</p>\n<br>\n<p>Honorable Mention: Aaron Jones for Patrick Mahomes (<em>Geraldo</em>)</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Most Active GM: </strong><em>Chorazyczewski</em> made 42 acquisitions and 4 trades this season totaling a league high 46 moves. <em>Steinmetz</em> and <em>Banchiu</em> were the biggest traders this season with 7 deals, almost a third of all the trades. Overall we had the same number of trades last year at 22.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Least Active GM: </strong><em>Laethem</em> was once again the least active manager with 19 total moves, 16 acquisitions and 3 trades (3 more than he made last year). He did have 3 more acquisitions than the lowest team, <em>Banchiu</em> and <em>Steinmetz</em>, who only made 13 acquisitions electing to make their transactions via trade.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>GM of the Year: </strong><em>Smith</em> is clearly the GM of the year. An argument could be made for <em>Butler</em> who made the playoffs for the first time ever and led the league in scoring, but <em>Smith</em> went from worst to first and set his team up for a potential dynasty. He secured draft capital and a strong core to build around last season, then added Saquon and JK Dobbins in the draft (we\u2019ll ignore Deebo Samuel). <em>Smith</em> had a plan and made pragmatic moves including his trade for Baker Mayfield to ensure he had his best team every Sunday.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">Drunkin Polaks</h2>\n<br>\n<p><em>Chorazyczewski</em> was in tank mode early and made some strong trades taking advantage of a drunk <em>Willen</em> a couple times to get lots of draft capital for the future. He also traded for George Pickens, who had a breakout year, and drafted Brock Bowers, who looks like the real deal. However, drafting Jonathan Brooks ahead of Brian Thomas and Keon Coleman before Ladd McConkey may set back <em>Chorazyczewski</em>\u2019s championship dreams for a fewI  years.</p>\n<br>\n<p>Way too early 2025 prediction: Tank mode</p>\n<br>\n<p>Key Acquisitions:</p>\n<br>\n<p>George Pickens</p>\n<br>\n<p>Brock Bowers</p>\n<br>\n<p>Keon Coleman</p>\n<br>\n<p>Jonathan Brooks</p>\n<br>\n<p>2024 Trade Chart:</p>\n<br>\n<table class=\"wu-table\">\n<tr><th>Kyle</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Sent</td><td>Received</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2024 1st (Trey Benson)</td><td>Braden's 2025 First (1.5)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>D'Andre Swift</td><td>Bobby's 2025 Second (2.11)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Travis Kelce</td><td>Bobby's 2026 First</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Mike Evans</td><td>Bobby's 2026 Second</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Jake Ferguson</td><td>Bobby's 2027 First</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Devin Singletary</td><td>George Pickens</td></tr>\n</table>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">Half Chubb</h2>\n<br>\n<p><em>Costello</em> was trying to build around Ja\u2019Marr Chase and CMC one last time and looked to have a really strong draft to do so. CMC had other plans that included not playing to start the season. <em>Costello</em>\u2019s team was reliant on CMC being a cheat code and had a slew of veterans that could put up reliable numbers. When it became evident that CMC wasn\u2019t going to do that he made some savvy moves trading Chase, CMC, and Cooper Kupp for 3 first rounders including getting his own pick back. In addition to the picks, <em>Costello</em> picked up strong rookie RBs on cheap deals in Tyrone Tracy and Braelon Allen.</p>\n<br>\n<p>Way too early 2025 prediction: Tank mode</p>\n<br>\n<p>Key Acquisitions:</p>\n<br>\n<p>Courtland Sutton</p>\n<br>\n<p>Tyrone Tracy</p>\n<br>\n<p>Braelon Allen</p>\n<br>\n<p>Nick Chubb</p>\n<br>\n<p>2024 Trade Chart:</p>\n<br>\n<table class=\"wu-table\">\n<tr><th>Mike</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Sent</td><td>Received</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ja'Marr Chase</td><td>Mike's 2025 First (1.2)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Christian McCaffrey</td><td>Jeff's 2025 First (1.8)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cooper Kupp</td><td>Campbell's 2025 Second (2.7)</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>Jeff's 2026 First</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>Braelon Allen</td></tr>\n</table>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">The Mixon Administration</h2>\n<br>\n<p><em>Laethem</em> rebuilt his roster before the season started with some strong moves. Aaron Jones via trade, George Kittle and Davante Adams in the auction and Xavier Worthy in the rookie draft gave him a chance to make the playoffs until he decided to improve his draft position towards the end of the season. Unlike last season, <em>Laethem</em> was more active this year making some trades and a few key pickups that will help him continue his rebuild. We will see which WRs will be reliable and if Caleb Williams can live up to his pedigree, but two picks in the upcoming rookie draft will give him a few more chances at building the foundation of his team. Taking Worthy over Thomas may prove to haunt <em>Laethem</em> for a few years.</p>\n<br>\n<p>Way too early 2025 prediction: Borderline playoff team</p>\n<br>\n<p>Key Acquisitions:</p>\n<br>\n<p>George Kittle</p>\n<br>\n<p>Xavier Worthy</p>\n<br>\n<p>Khalil Shakir</p>\n<br>\n<p>Josh Downs</p>\n<br>\n<p>Caleb Williams</p>\n<br>\n<p>Tank Bigsby</p>\n<br>\n<p>2024 Trade Chart:</p>\n<br>\n<table class=\"wu-table\">\n<tr><th>James</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Sent</td><td>Received</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Patrick Mahomes</td><td>2024 1st (Caleb Williams)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Calvin Ridley</td><td>Bobby's 2025 First (1.11)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Baker Mayfield</td><td>Josh Downs</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>Khalil Shakir</td></tr>\n</table>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">The Nixon Administration</h2>\n<br>\n<p>Wait, didn\u2019t we just do this team? Not so fast, my friend, <em>Frink</em> added QB of the future Bo Nix and updated his team name to reflect the new regime. This enabled him to move Joe Burrow for more picks and add another cheap, young keeper. Along with Chubba Hubbard from last year, Bucky Irving will help <em>Frink</em> have a young core to compete for the playoffs next year. Adding in a couple key vets in Andrews and Jeudy will add some much needed depth to backfill Jameson Williams who will be a RFA this offseason. In order to compete next year he needs to avoid poor auction moves again next year (Najee Harris and Zamir White were not it) to compete in 2025.</p>\n<br>\n<p>Way too early 2025 prediction: Borderline playoff team</p>\n<br>\n<p>Key Acquisitions:</p>\n<br>\n<p>Bo Nix</p>\n<br>\n<p>Bucky Irving</p>\n<br>\n<p>Jerry Jeudy</p>\n<br>\n<p>Mark Andrews</p>\n<br>\n<p>2024 Trade Chart:</p>\n<br>\n<table class=\"wu-table\">\n<tr><th>Collin</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Sent</td><td>Received</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Collin's 2026 Second</td><td>Jeff's 2025 Second (2.8)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Joe Burrow</td><td>Jeff's 2026 Second</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>Mark Andrews</td></tr>\n</table>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">Show Me Your TDs</h2>\n<br>\n<p><em>Geraldo</em> failed to make the playoffs for the first time since 2014 and only the second time in league history. Early season injuries made it an uphill battle, which he fell short of in the end. However, he was able to rebuild on the fly adding in two rookie every week starters in Daniels and McConkey, as well as some vets on cheap deals. <em>Geraldo</em> let go of some of his long-time vet favorites in Aaron Jones and Stefon Diggs for basically nothing, but will look to fill the gaps with the extra FAAB money he won for winning the losers bracket.</p>\n<br>\n<p>Way too early 2025 prediction: Championship contender</p>\n<br>\n<p>Key Acquisitions:</p>\n<br>\n<p>Jayden Daniels</p>\n<br>\n<p>Ladd McConkey</p>\n<br>\n<p>Tony Pollard</p>\n<br>\n<p>Jaylen Warren</p>\n<br>\n<p>Rashid Shaheed</p>\n<br>\n<p>Michael Pittman</p>\n<br>\n<p>2024 Trade Chart:</p>\n<br>\n<table class=\"wu-table\">\n<tr><th>Braden</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Sent</td><td>Received</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Braden's 2026 Second</td><td>2024 2nd (Ladd McConkey)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Aaron Jones</td><td>Michael Pittman</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Rico Dowdle</td><td>Patrick Mahomes</td></tr>\n</table>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">Lets Cook</h2>\n<br>\n<p><em>Banchiu</em> and <em>Steinmetz</em> once again made some major trades, first to improve their starting lineup trading a top 3 pick and some other pieces for Derrick Henry, then built for the future by trading DK Metcalf for JSN and the pick that turned into Caleb Williams for Calvin Ridley. All were tremendous moves to help them win now and build for the future. During the season they went all-in by trading another top 5 pick for Mike Evans and Travis Kelce, which did not pan out. They were still able to salvage their future late in the season, while costing themselves a playoff spot, by flipping Kamara for a late first in the 2025 draft and Kelce for Laporta. They will once again be straddling a playoff run and a rebuild next year.</p>\n<br>\n<p>Way too early 2025 prediction: Borderline playoff team</p>\n<br>\n<p>Key Acquisitions:</p>\n<br>\n<p>Jaxon Smith-Njigba</p>\n<br>\n<p>Derrick Henry</p>\n<br>\n<p>Calvin Ridley</p>\n<br>\n<p>Sam Laporta</p>\n<br>\n<p>Sam Darnold</p>\n<br>\n<p>Cade Otton</p>\n<br>\n<p>2024 Trade Chart:</p>\n<br>\n<table class=\"wu-table\">\n<tr><th>Adam & Stein</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Sent</td><td>Received</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2024 1st (Caleb Williams)</td><td>Brad's 2025 First (1.10)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Mike's 2025 First (1.2)</td><td>Collin's 2026 Second</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Braden's 2025 First (1.5)</td><td>Calvin Ridley</td></tr>\n<tr><td>DK Metcalf</td><td>Jaxon Smith-Njigba</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Diontae Johnson</td><td>Derrick Henry</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Gus Edwards</td><td>Rashid Shaheed</td></tr>\n<tr><td>George Pickens</td><td>Mike Evans</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Mark Andrews</td><td>Jordan Love</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Alvin Kamara</td><td>Sam Laporta</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Jared Goff</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Travis Kelce</td><td></td></tr>\n</table>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">Windy City Waterboys</h2>\n<br>\n<p><em>Gillespie</em> snuck into the playoffs after losing five of his last six regular season games. Gibbs and ARSB remained elite options and the additions of Jacobs and Higgins were strong moves, but the lack of top-tier QB and some tough lineup decisions ultimately cost him a shot at his first championship. <em>Gillespie</em> still has some great deals with Gibbs entering the final year of his rookie deal to go with the cheap vets ARSB, McBride, and Higgins, but the time is ticking on this iteration of his team. No rookie picks last year or this year will force <em>Gillespie</em> to build through the auction and free agency again.</p>\n<br>\n<p>Way too early 2025 prediction: championship contender again</p>\n<br>\n<p>Key Acquisitions:</p>\n<br>\n<p>Tee Higgins</p>\n<br>\n<p>Jauan Jennings</p>\n<br>\n<p>Josh Jacobs</p>\n<br>\n<p>Jordan Mason</p>\n<br>\n<p>2024 Trade Chart:</p>\n<br>\n<table class=\"wu-table\">\n<tr><th>Campbell</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Sent</td><td>Received</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2024 2nd (JJ McCarthy)</td><td>Braden's 2026 Second</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Campbell's 2025 Second (2.7)</td><td>Christian Watson</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Michael Pittman</td><td>Jauan Jennings</td></tr>\n</table>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">Benches Don\u2019t Score Points I Do</h2>\n<br>\n<p><em>Butler</em> wasn\u2019t leaving his hopes for ending his playoff drought up to chance and made some bold all-in moves before the season started by adding James Cook and Justin Jefferson. He followed those moves up with additional now or never moves by trading his next two drafts away to stack Joe Burrow and Ja\u2019Marr Chase, both of whom he could keep at about market value. <em>Butler</em> successfully made the playoffs, but put up a dud and was eliminated without a playoff win. The goalpost has shifted slightly and <em>Butler</em> joins <em>Frink</em> as the only managers without a playoff victory to their names. The journey to win a playoff game will be tougher, even though he has pieces to build around he has few assets to trade next season to take his fate into his own hands again.</p>\n<br>\n<p>Way too early 2025 prediction: Playoff contender, but still no playoff wins</p>\n<br>\n<p>Key Acquisitions:</p>\n<br>\n<p>Ja\u2019Marr Chase</p>\n<br>\n<p>Joe Burrow</p>\n<br>\n<p>James Cook</p>\n<br>\n<p>Darnell Mooney</p>\n<br>\n<p>DK Metcalf</p>\n<br>\n<p>2024 Trade Chart:</p>\n<br>\n<table class=\"wu-table\">\n<tr><th>Jeff</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Sent</td><td>Received</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2024 1st (Malik Nabers)</td><td>2024 2nd (JJ McCarthy)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Jeff's 2025 First (1.8)</td><td>Justin Jefferson</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Jeff's 2025 Second (2.8)</td><td>James Cook</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Jeff's 2026 First</td><td>DK Metcalf</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Jeff's 2026 Second</td><td>Ja'Marr Chase</td></tr>\n<tr><td>DJ Moore</td><td>Joe Burrow</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Christian Watson</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Jaxon Smith-Njigba</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Braelon Allen</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Sam Laporta</td><td></td></tr>\n</table>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">Can\u2019t REED Can\u2019t Write</h2>\n<br>\n<p><em>Keaton</em> entered the season with a similar team to his Championship squad from the year prior, and made one big trade to improve his second RB spot with Aaron Jones and added Terry McLaurin which caused more headaches than helped win games. Overall <em>Keaton</em>\u2019s championship defense will go down as a failure after losing in the first round of the playoffs, though he did win the Thunderdome division. Garrett Wilson and Isiah Pacheco are going to be RFAs and Josh Allen and CeeDee Lamb get a little more expensive, but Kyren Williams remains cheap so he should be able to bring back the core team again. With no draft picks again, the bubble may burst soon.</p>\n<br>\n<p>Way too early 2025 prediction: championship contender</p>\n<br>\n<p>Key Acquisitions:</p>\n<br>\n<p>Aaron Jones</p>\n<br>\n<p>Terry McLaurin</p>\n<br>\n<p>David Njoku</p>\n<br>\n<p>Tua Tagovailoa</p>\n<br>\n<p>2024 Trade Chart:</p>\n<br>\n<table class=\"wu-table\">\n<tr><th>Blake</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Sent</td><td>Received</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Bobby's 2025 First (1.11)</td><td>Aaron Jones</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Khalil Shakir</td><td>David Njoku</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cedric Tillman</td><td></td></tr>\n</table>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">Detroit Lions Defense</h2>\n<br>\n<p>After a surprisingly hot start, <em>White</em> had to recalibrate his rebuild strategy to a win-now approach. He traded Derrick Henry before the season and got what would have been a top 3 pick in 2025, but when he started 5-2 he became a buyer. <em>White</em> flipped the top 3 pick to <em>Costello</em> for CMC and Cooper Kupp and later moved his 2025 first rounder for Alvin Kamara to make his team look unbeatable entering the playoffs. However, some tough injury luck starting with CMC and ending with Jalen Hurts going down early in the second round of the playoffs ended <em>White</em>\u2019s hope to win his fifth championship. He revamped his team and added some veterans that he could potentially keep and try for another run, but no draft picks this year will keep him kicking a rebuild down the road.</p>\n<br>\n<p>Way too early 2025 prediction: Playoff hopeful</p>\n<br>\n<p>Key Acquisitions:</p>\n<br>\n<p>Alvin Kamara</p>\n<br>\n<p>Cooper Kupp</p>\n<br>\n<p>Christian McCaffrey</p>\n<br>\n<p>Jonnu Smith</p>\n<br>\n<p>Adam Thielen</p>\n<br>\n<p>Tucker Kraft</p>\n<br>\n<p>Jared Goff</p>\n<br>\n<p>Kareem Hunt</p>\n<br>\n<p>Jalen McMillan</p>\n<br>\n<p>2024 Trade Chart:</p>\n<br>\n<table class=\"wu-table\">\n<tr><th>Brad</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Sent</td><td>Received</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Brad's 2025 First (1.10)</td><td>Christian McCaffrey</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Derrick Henry</td><td>Cooper Kupp</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Rashid Shaheed</td><td>Diontae Johnson</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>Gus Edwards</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>Alvin Kamara</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>Jared Goff</td></tr>\n</table>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">There\u2019s a YOU Sheriff In Town</h2>\n<br>\n<p><em>Willen</em> was one start/sit decision away from his first title in just his second year of the league. He made some wild moves, giving up his first round picks until 2028. Adding Malik Nabers will help bridge any gaps and he still has Bijan Robinson for cheap for one more year along with Nico Collins and Puca Nacua locked in for a while. Lamar Jackson was a key auction pick this offseason and helped take his team to the championship game. His team should be competitive for at least one more year, but a running back issue is looming for <em>Willen</em>.</p>\n<br>\n<p>Way too early 2025 prediction: Championship contender</p>\n<br>\n<p>Key Acquisitions:</p>\n<br>\n<p>Malik Nabers</p>\n<br>\n<p>D\u2019Andre Swift</p>\n<br>\n<p>DJ Moore</p>\n<p>Jake Ferguson</p>\n<br>\n<p>2024 Trade Chart:</p>\n<br>\n<table class=\"wu-table\">\n<tr><th>Bobby</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Sent</td><td>Received</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2024 1st (Malik Nabers)</td><td>2024 1st (Malik Nabers)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Bobby's 2025 Second (2.11)</td><td>2024 2nd (Trey Benson)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Bobby's 2026 First</td><td>DJ Moore</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Bobby's 2026 Second</td><td>D'Andre Swift</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Bobby's 2027 First</td><td>Jake Ferguson</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Justin Jefferson</td><td>Devin Singletary</td></tr>\n<tr><td>James Cook</td><td></td></tr>\n</table>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">The Benchwarmers</h2>\n<br>\n<p><em>Smith</em> took his draft picks and added one mega star (Brian Thomas) and a couple projects (Marvin Harrison and Rome Odunze), then spent a majority of his FAAB on the fantasy MVP Saquon Barkley which paid off big. Drake London, Jordan Addison, and Chase Brown were three Key Acquisitions last year that set him up for success this year. QB will be a question next year with Anthony Richardson not showing promise as a passer, but the late season move to get Baker could help alleviate those concerns as <em>Smith</em> shifts from rebuild to championship defense mode.</p>\n<br>\n<p>Way too early 2025 prediction: Repeat championship contender</p>\n<br>\n<p>Key Acquisitions:</p>\n<br>\n<p>Saquon Barkley</p>\n<br>\n<p>Brian Thomas Jr.</p>\n<br>\n<p>Marvin Harrison Jr.</p>\n<br>\n<p>Rome Odunze</p>\n<br>\n<p>JK Dobbins</p>\n<br>\n<p>Baker Mayfield</p>\n<br>\n<p>2024 Trade Chart:</p>\n<br>\n<table class=\"wu-table\">\n<tr><th>Casey</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Sent</td><td>Received</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2024 Second (Ladd McConkey)</td><td>Rico Dowdle</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Josh Downs</td><td>Baker Mayfield</td></tr>\n</table>\n<br>\n<p class=\"wu-bold\">_______________</p>"},{"title":"2024 Pre-Season Preview","year":"2024","html":"<p>2024 Pre-Season Preview</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">Championship Contenders:</h2>\n<br>\n<h3 class=\"wu-h3\">Two Wilson\u2019s One Kupp</h3>\n<br>\n<p>Returns most of his team from his championship team there is no reason to see a major drop off. The big risks are a drop off of Josh Allen without receivers or Kyren Williams Cinderella run from last year coming to an end. However, his depth is good enough to keep him competitive. He also has only one Wilson and no Kupps on his team, so a new team name would help his prospects.</p>\n<br>\n<h3 class=\"wu-h3\">There\u2019s a YOU Sheriff in Town</h3>\n<br>\n<p>All in to win this year and his roster is strong at the top with depth to withstand injuries. He made the championship game last year in his first full year in charge and I can see him making it again. The biggest concern is tight end and the shift in his receivers situations.</p>\n<br>\n<h3 class=\"wu-h3\">Show Me Your TDs</h3>\n<br>\n<p>Always a championship contender, this year is no different. The top of his roster remains stacked with JT, Tyreek, and Achane and is significantly better to start this season than last. Risks to his run will be a major fall off of Diggs or an injury to one of his studs.</p>\n<br>\n<h3 class=\"wu-h3\">Windy City Waterboys</h3>\n<br>\n<p>A very deep team with some star power at the top. He had a real shot depending how things break to win it all. The biggest risk is at quarterback and his elite guys are not quite at the highest level at their positions. Either way a definite contender again this year.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">Playoff hopefuls:</h2>\n<br>\n<h3 class=\"wu-h3\">What Can Brown do for You?</h3>\n<br>\n<p>While his team isn\u2019t a contender for the first time in a long time, he is at the top of the playoff hopefuls. He still has AJ Brown and Hurts so he will be competitive, but his starting lineup is not as locked in as years past.</p>\n<br>\n<h3 class=\"wu-h3\">Benches Don\u2019t Score Points I Do</h3>\n<br>\n<p>On paper his team is a championship contender, but I don\u2019t have it in me to put this all-time LOSER in that tier. With that said he has a strong starting lineup with minimal depth (hence the new name), what could go wrong? \ud83e\udd74</p>\n<br>\n<h3 class=\"wu-h3\">Chasing Dimes</h3>\n<br>\n<p>Looking like the end of a strong contending period, his starting lineup already has more Qs than an Invesco ETF. Ja\u2019Marr will likely play at some point, CMC probably isn\u2019t hurt that bad, but Chubb won\u2019t be back until at least week 4 so a slow start could be damning.</p>\n<br>\n<h3 class=\"wu-h3\">The Benchwarmers</h3>\n<br>\n<p>A strong rookie draft and two great free agent pick ups put him ahead of his rebuilding schedule. Richardson and his second running back will be the big difference in his season (up or down), but a surprisingly solid looking team.</p>\n<br>\n<h3 class=\"wu-h3\">The Mixon Administration</h3>\n<br>\n<p>His starting lineup is solid, but not spectacular. The depth at running back gives him flexibility to make a trade to get a future pick or package a couple players for an upgrade depending on how his season starts.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">College football fans:</h2>\n<br>\n<h3 class=\"wu-h3\">Let\u2019s Cook</h3>\n<br>\n<p>They have a good team, but they lack a truly elite player. Maybe Henry or Kamara can relive their glory days, but it\u2019s tough to see the weekly upside of this team. I see them winning a little early but falling short as the season progresses.</p>\n<br>\n<h3 class=\"wu-h3\">Drunkin Polaks</h3>\n<br>\n<p>He has an interesting young team, but tough to see a path to success this year. With that said he will beat some teams that he\u2019s not supposed to and have some big boom weeks. The tank has seemingly already started with a pre-season trade for a couple of picks.</p>\n<br>\n<h3 class=\"wu-h3\">Burrow Mckokiner</h3>\n<br>\n<p>The direction here is unclear. Last year he traded for Mostert, but traded away Kyren Williams who is younger. His free agent draft was for a team trying to win now, but then he put himself in a gauntlet of a division. With two first round picks already next year I expect at some point he\u2019ll try to add another during the season setting him up for future success.</p>\n<br>\n<p class=\"wu-bold\">_______________</p>\n<br>\n<p class=\"wu-bold\">2024 Draft Rankings:</p>\n<br>\n<li>Bobby - Nabers and Benson are strong incoming rookies and he was able to bring back Etienne. While Jackson was a bit of an overpay with Dak on his roster, he was the best available QB and was a decent price.</li>\n<br>\n<li>Kyle - Bowers and Brooks were the best rookies at their position and Keon Coleman was a solid add late in the first. He only had two spots available and nailed both picks as the only player to spend all his money and fill his roster with Kelce and Mike Evans</li>\n<br>\n<li>Casey - with 5 picks it would have been hard to him to mess it up, and he definitely didn\u2019t. Harrison, Odunze, and Brian Thomas are all strong draft picks with upside today and into the future. Smith took a couple of handcuff backs who could have value this year. In the free agent draft he was able to add the top RB and a strong starting WR in Saquon and Deebo along with his favorite oft injured RB, Dobbins for cheap.</li>\n<br>\n<li>James - a strong rookie draft of Worthy, Caleb Williams, and Ricky Pearsall started his day off right. In the free agent draft James added a starting WR, Davante Adams, and the second best available TE, Kittle, for relatively cheap. He was also able to bring back Javonte Williams who should be a solid starter along with Hopkins and Watson returning home.</li>\n<br>\n<li>Campbell - he only had one second round pick and got a decent hand cuff option in Braelon Allen. Where he shined was the free agent auction. Jacobs and Waddle were both decent values and Higgins was a great value. Campbell bolstered n already talented roster.</li>\n<br>\n<li>Collin - he only had two late second round picks but managed to get two players in Adonai Mitchell and Bucky Irving who project to have a role and could be more than that. He continued to get great value with Devonta Smith, but took a couple heat checks with Najee Harris and Zamir white who are solid but not special. The tequila high noon must\u2019ve hit when Kendre Millers name got called because that was a huge overpay\u2026 but Schultz was a good pick up to solidify that spot.</li>\n<br>\n<li>Braden - he turned a late first and early second pick into a potential starting QB and a should have been first round WR in Jayden Daniels and Ladd McConkey. In the free agent draft he was able to bring back Pollard on a cheaper deal and add Ekeler but nothing to write home about.</li>\n<br>\n<li>Mike - he had no picks and his back against the wall and managed to bring home a complete team. Ja\u2019marr Chase was a great value at only $57. He added Kupp for $18 and Chubb for $24 who might help him late in the season but could be a keeper for the rest of his career. He also got depth at WR and a couple solid QBs to fill out the roster.</li>\n<br>\n<li>Blake - with no picks he was able to add solid value with Terry McLaurin. He added a couple of solid TEs and brought back Lawrence along with some cheap options with potential roles in Shakir and Chandler.</li>\n<br>\n<li>Adam/Matt - they had two middle second round picks and got and got a first round WR in Xavier Legette who has questions and a RB who is third or forth on the depth chart in Jaylen Wright. They got good value in Amari Cooper and the 49ers D but didn\u2019t add a top tier talent they really needed</li>\n<br>\n<li>Brad - he drafted Marshawn Lloyd who may or may not be involved this year. He also added some solid values with Lockett and bringing Goedert back but didn\u2019t have enough money to make a big splash</li>\n<br>\n<li>Jeff - he drafted three QBs, one of which will start, with his three second round picks and cut JJ McCarthy. Other than that I barely remember him there.</li>\n<br>\n<p class=\"wu-bold\">_______________</p>\n<br>\n<p>**</p>"},{"title":"2023 Year in Review","year":"2023","html":"<p>2023 Year in Review**</p>\n<br>\n<p>It is time to reflect as we approach the turn of the new year. The 2023 season was by far the most exciting we\u2019ve had. There were more trades than ever and many new faces at the top of the playoff bracket. This season we are guaranteed to have a first time MSUFFL champion for the second time in three seasons. One thing that we can count on is <em>Butler</em> missed the playoffs for the 12th straight year. The more things change, the more they stay the same. Now let\u2019s get into the fun stuff\u2026 I\u2019ll start by giving out some awards for the season and give my expert, unbiased, opinion on everyone\u2019s future outlook along with some notes on the season in review.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Best 2023 Keeper Value:</strong> Christian McCaffrey - the most consistently great running back at a reasonable $62 price point entering this year. He gives <em>Costello</em> a chance to win every week on his own.</p>\n<br>\n<p>Honorable Mention: Tyreek Hill</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Best Auction Value: </strong>Raheem Mostert - a depth pickup at the end of the auction draft, <em>Smith</em> was able to turn a $2 player into <em>Frink</em>\u2019s 2024 first round pick which ended up being number three overall. Mostert was the second best RB in terms of fantasy points and will be a value into next year if he keeps a similar role.</p>\n<br>\n<p>Honorable Mention: CeeDee Lamb</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Best Rookie Draft Pick:</strong> Jamhyr Gibbs - it was an easy pick for <em>Gillespie</em> at the time, but if the draft were to happen again today, Gibbs would have potentially gone with the first pick given his immediate impact. He has the added bonus of being the home town kid.</p>\n<br>\n<p>Honorable Mention: Sam Laporta</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Best Free Agent Pickup:</strong> The Rams (Puka Nacua and Kyren Williams) - it\u2019s difficult to pick one so I\u2019m not going to. Both were great pickups that were instant automatic starters. Nacua picked up by <em>Willen</em> for $10 has made his team a contender this year and gives him a forever keeper. Williams picked up by <em>Frink</em> and traded to <em>Keaton</em> for a 2024 second round pick and a 2025 first round pick provided value for both parties.</p>\n<br>\n<p>Honorable Mentions: De\u2019Von Achane, CJ Stroud, Tank Dell, Trey McBride</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Best Trade: </strong>Rachaad White and a 2025 first for Nico Collins - at the time this trade looked pretty balanced, and even in hindsight it is not a bad trade for <em>Willen</em>. After the trade was made White started performing like a RB1, which would mean the 2025 first round pick is just icing on the cake for <em>Keaton</em>.</p>\n<br>\n<p>Honorable Mention: Collin\u2019s 2024 first and second round picks (#3) for Mostert (<em>Smith</em>)</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Worst Keeper Value:</strong> Miles Sanders - <em>Frink</em> kept Sanders for $42 so he felt obligated to start him almost every week through week 10 and he only scored double digits once. Not only is the opportunity cost high, but Sanders cost <em>Frink</em> games including week 8 when Sanders scored 0 points. Woof.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Worst Rookie Draft Pick: </strong>Quentin Johnston - his rookie season was terrible especially when you consider Mike Williams got injured early on leaving a potential opportunity for Johnston. <em>Butler</em> passed on a few others that could have been future cornerstones for him most notably would be Achane. Richardson and Charbonnet would have both been better picks considering it was a lost year for <em>Butler</em> and both of them flashed game changing potential in a limited sample.</p>\n<br>\n<p>Honorable Mention: Kendre Miller over Jayden Reed</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Worst Trade: </strong>Austin Ekeler - any trade for Ekeler this year was bad. First <em>Geraldo</em> traded away Rhamondre Stevenson and Terry McLaurin along with both of his first rounders in 2024 and 2025 for him. Then <em>Gillespie</em> made the same mistake on tilt trading Pollard and his 2024 and 2025 first rounders for Ekeler. Now that Herbert is out and the Chargers are imploding, Ekeler isn\u2019t even starter worthy in the fantasy playoffs and his career outlook is looking bleak.</p>\n<br>\n<p>Honorable Mention: Bobby\u2019s 2024 first and Drake London for Christian Kirk and D\u2019onta Foreman (<em>Willen</em>)</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Most Active GM: </strong><em>Geraldo</em> made 45 acquisitions and 9 trades this season. The averages were 24 and 4 with the second highest in acquisitions, <em>Smith</em>, had 33 and in trades, <em>Chorazyczewski and Banchiu/Steinmetz, </em>had 6. Hopefully with a child next year he can use his attention for something more productive.</p>\n<br>\n<p>Honorable Mentions: <em>Costello</em> and <em>Smith</em></p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Least Active GM: </strong><em>Laethem</em> made 12 acquisitions and was the only owner not involved in a trade. He has 3 more acquisitions than the lowest team, <em>Banchiu/Steinmetz</em>, who only made 9 electing to make their transactions via trade.</p>\n<br>\n<p>Honorable Mention: <em>Butler</em></p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>GM of the Year: </strong><em>Keaton</em> used his available resources intelligently from the draft to some key in-season trades. He put himself in position to be the favorite for a championship (what could go wrong) and ended up leading the league in scoring on top of securing a first round bye. His only rookie pick was Jayden Reed in the second round. Nico Collins, George Kittle, and CeeDee Lamb were all great auction values and his three trades for Cooper Kupp, Rachaad White, and Kyren Williams have put him in position to win.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">The Benchwarmers</h2>\n<br>\n<p><em>Smith</em> has a lot to look forward to this offseason. He has four first round picks this year including two of the top three. Anthony Richardson should come back fully healthy and in just four games he flashed his Josh Allen/Cam Newton like upside, but missed a game due to injury before his season ending injury. Jordan Addison and Drake London should be solid starters next year on rookie salaries but RB is going to be a question mark unless one of the rookies he acquired this season can win the lead back role this offseason.</p>\n<br>\n<p>Way too early 2024/25 prediction: Still in tanking mode</p>\n<br>\n<p>Key FA Acquisitions:</p>\n<br>\n<p>Keaton Mitchell - $0</p>\n<br>\n<p>Chase Brown - $1</p>\n<br>\n<p>Josh Downs - $2</p>\n<br>\n<p>Dontayvion Wicks - $0</p>\n<br>\n<p>Demario Douglas - $0</p>\n<br>\n<p>Tommy DeVito - lol jk</p>\n<br>\n<p>2023 Trade Chart:</p>\n<br>\n<table class=\"wu-table\">\n<tr><th>Casey</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Sent</td><td>Received</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Tua Tagovailoa</td><td>Collin's 2024 First</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Raheem Mostert</td><td>Brad's 2024 First</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Alvin Kamara</td><td>Bobby's 2024 First</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Mike Evans</td><td>Adam/Matt's 2025 First</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Christian Kirk</td><td>Jordan Addison</td></tr>\n<tr><td>D\u2019onta Foreman</td><td>Drake London</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>Roschon Johnson</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>Collin's 2024 Second</td></tr>\n</table>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">Breece\u2019s Pierces</h2>\n<br>\n<p><em>Butler</em> had high hopes entering the year until he started 0-4 barely breaking 100 once. At that point he went back in the tank. Key injuries to Nick Chubb, Christian Watson, and I guess Aaron Rogers (if he\u2019s still good) definitely hurt <em>Butler</em>\u2019s chances of turning his franchise around. The saving grace is the core members of his team \u2013 Breece Hall, Watson, Chris Olave, and Sam Laporta \u2013 are still young, shown flashes of talent, and cheap. He only has one first rounder but should be able to get another difference maker at two overall and two more second rounders to try and find a diamond in the rough. If he can hit with a QB and some difference makers to fill in the gaps he will be in a much better position for next season.</p>\n<br>\n<p>Way too early 2024/25 prediction: Frisky, but no playoffs</p>\n<br>\n<p>Key FA Acquisitions:</p>\n<br>\n<p>DJ Moore - $37</p>\n<br>\n<p>2023 Trade Chart:</p>\n<br>\n<table class=\"wu-table\">\n<tr><th>Jeff</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Sent</td><td>Received</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Darren Waller</td><td>Mike's 2024 Second</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>Michael Thomas</td></tr>\n</table>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">Burrow Mckokiner</h2>\n<br>\n<p><em>Frink</em> made strong moves this year to win-now, unfortunately he forgot to win. He picked up Kyren Williams after a strong week 1 outing and traded for Raheem Mostert, who had a fantastic year, but those two players alone were not able to carry his team. <em>Frink</em> has an interesting offseason ahead of him as Mostert is cheap but old and his WRs outside of Jameson Williams and Zay Flowers are all borderline keepers. He also has no 2024 first round picks and potentially the last two second round picks, so rookie help is unlikely.</p>\n<br>\n<p>Way too early 2024/25 prediction: Must tank</p>\n<br>\n<p>Key FA Acquisitions:</p>\n<br>\n<p>Brandon Aiyuk - $25</p>\n<br>\n<p>Chuba Hubbard - $0</p>\n<br>\n<p>Jonathan Mingo - $0</p>\n<br>\n<p>2023 Trade Chart:</p>\n<br>\n<table class=\"wu-table\">\n<tr><th>Collin</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Sent</td><td>Received</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Collin's 2024 First</td><td>Blake's 2025 First</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Kyren Williams</td><td>Raheem Mostert</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Marquise Brown</td><td>Blake's 2024 second</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Collin's 2024 Second</td><td>Bobby's 2024 Second</td></tr>\n</table>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">Lets Cook</h2>\n<br>\n<p><em>Banchiu</em> and <em>Steinmetz</em> decided their team needed a major facelift and you can see by the trade chart below they achieved that. They turned Lamar into Tua, Ekeler into Kamara, Montgomery into James Connor, Roschon into Stevenson, and added a couple receivers and second rounders in the process. They got cheaper while upgrading their depth, which in a lost season would be considered a win. I expect they will keep a large portion of the team into next year and hope for a better outcome</p>\n<br>\n<p>Way too early 2024/25 prediction: Playoff hopeful</p>\n<br>\n<p>Key FA Acquisitions:</p>\n<br>\n<p>Deandre Hopkins - $24</p>\n<br>\n<p>Gus Edwards - $10</p>\n<br>\n<p>2023 Trade Chart:</p>\n<br>\n<table class=\"wu-table\">\n<tr><th>Adam/Matt</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Sent</td><td>Received</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Adam/Matt's 2024 First</td><td>Braden's 2024 First</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Adam/Matt's 2025 First</td><td>Braden's 2025 First</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Lamar Jackson</td><td>Mike's 2025 First</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Austin Ekeler</td><td>Tua Tagovailoa</td></tr>\n<tr><td>David Montgomery</td><td>Alvin Kamara</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Roschon Johnson</td><td>James Conner</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Evan Engram</td><td>Rhamondre Stevenson</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>George Pickens</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>Terry McLaurin</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>Braden's 2024 Second</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>Kyle's 2024 Second</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>Braden's 2025 Second</td></tr>\n</table>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">Drunkin Polaks</h2>\n<br>\n<p><em>Chorazyczewski</em> got a lot of slack for his lack of strategy early in the season as wins piled up while he was making tanking moves. While a team that had CMC and Cooper Kupp on it last season never won the championship, <em>Chorazyczewski</em> might be in the best position of any team not in the playoffs this season to build a true dynasty due to some fantastic free agent pickups. Rashee Rice, CJ Stroud, and Tank Dell all appear to be real players and all have cheap salaries even compared to their 2023 performances. Brian Robinson was the big splash trade <em>Chorazyczewski</em> made to start the season and it turned out to be a savvy move. He\u2019ll have three first round picks in 2024 (included one from the CMC trade) to add to his young and cheap core and should have plenty of FAAB to win some key vets in the auction.</p>\n<br>\n<p>Way too early 2024/25 prediction: Playoff hopeful</p>\n<br>\n<p>Key FA Acquisitions:</p>\n<br>\n<p>Rashee Rice - $1</p>\n<br>\n<p>CJ Stroud - $0</p>\n<br>\n<p>Tank Dell - $3</p>\n<br>\n<p>2023 Trade Chart:</p>\n<br>\n<table class=\"wu-table\">\n<tr><th>Kyle</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Sent</td><td>Received</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Kirk Cousins</td><td>Adam/Matt's 2024 First</td></tr>\n<tr><td>James Conner</td><td>Blake's 2024 First</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cooper Kupp</td><td>Brian Robinson</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2023 1st (Jordan Addison)</td><td>Justin Tucker</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Kyle's 2024 Second</td><td>Antonio Gibson</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>Jakobi Meyers</td></tr>\n</table>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">The Mixon Administration</h2>\n<br>\n<p><em>Laethem</em> made a couple key acquisitions early, Keenan Allen in the auction and Adam Theilen in free agency, to set his team in a good position with a 5-2 start to the season. However his reliance on the Chiefs passing offense began to betray him and Theilen also showed his age in the second half of the season. Kelce and Mixon are starting to get expensive and show their age, and Mahomes didn\u2019t pay off his value as the most expensive QB this season. Lucky for <em>Laethem</em> he still has his first this year, but he failed to add anyone young and exciting in free agency this season outside of maybe Jerome Ford.</p>\n<br>\n<p>Way too early 2024/25 prediction: Embrace the tank</p>\n<br>\n<p>Key FA Acquisitions:</p>\n<br>\n<p>Keenan Allen - $26</p>\n<br>\n<p>Adam Theilen - $2</p>\n<br>\n<p>Jerome Ford - $3</p>\n<br>\n<p>2023 Trade Chart:</p>\n<br>\n<p>N/A</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">What Can Brown Do For You?</h2>\n<br>\n<p>After starting 6-2, <em>White</em> barely made the playoffs going 1-5 the rest of the way. The Brad-adelphia Eagles super stack began to rear (pun-intended) it\u2019s ugly head as the tush push was removing value from AJ Brown and Dallas Goedert. <em>White</em> struggled to find a second RB all season until a late move to get David Montgomery settled that position down a little. If Derrick Henry and Davante Adams are past their expiration dates as elite fantasy players, the <em>White</em> reign might be over. Unfortunately for his future outlook he traded away his 2024 first and George Pickens (a potential backfill for Adams) for Montgomery, so rebuilding next season may be necessary. He did however make a few moves in free agency that could add some depth or potentially give him some time to inject some more talent into his roster.</p>\n<br>\n<p>Way too early 2024/25 prediction: Playoff hopeful</p>\n<br>\n<p>Key FA Acquisitions:</p>\n<br>\n<p>Zeke Elliott - $3</p>\n<br>\n<p>Rashid Shaheed - $0</p>\n<br>\n<p>Joshua Palmer - $6</p>\n<br>\n<p>Cole Kmet - $0</p>\n<br>\n<p>Jordan Love - $0</p>\n<br>\n<p>2023 Trade Chart:</p>\n<br>\n<table class=\"wu-table\">\n<tr><th>Brad</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Sent</td><td>Received</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Brad's 2024 First</td><td>David Montgomery</td></tr>\n<tr><td>George Pickens</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Justin Tucker</td><td></td></tr>\n</table>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">Show Me Your TDs</h2>\n<br>\n<p><em>Geraldo</em> struggled all year with injuries and barely made the playoffs largely on the back of a crazy Tyreek Hill season, only to be struck by an injury to Hill himself. Jonathan Taylor missed 7 games, Aaron Jones missed 6, De\u2019von Achane missed 5, and Justin Fields missed 4. He made plenty of moves to try and figure out a way to stay afloat including a bad decision to go all-in on Ekeler, but was later bailed out by <em>Gillespie</em> when he offered a lifeline to get Pollard and a couple of first round picks back. He also chased a TE solution like crazy making countless free agent pickups and two trades only to realize he should have just stuck with Njoku. That being said, he was able to pick up Achane and Tyjae Spears to bolster his RB room now and into the future and Purdy looks like a potential MVP in the 49ers system.</p>\n<br>\n<p>Way too early 2024/25 prediction: Championship contender</p>\n<br>\n<p>Key FA Acquisitions:</p>\n<br>\n<p>Jonathan Taylor - $47</p>\n<br>\n<p>De\u2019von Achane - $0</p>\n<br>\n<p>Brock Purdy - $10</p>\n<br>\n<p>2023 Trade Chart:</p>\n<br>\n<table class=\"wu-table\">\n<tr><th>Braden</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Sent</td><td>Received</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Braden's 2024 First</td><td>Campbell's 2024 First</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Braden's 2025 First</td><td>Campbell's 2025 first</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Rachaad White</td><td>Tony Pollard</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Rhamondre Stevenson</td><td>Bobby's 2nd (Tyjae Spears)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Terry McLaurin</td><td>Marquise Brown</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Braden's 2025 Second</td><td>Darren Waller</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Braden's 2024 Second</td><td>Evan Engram</td></tr>\n</table>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">Windy City Waterboys</h2>\n<br>\n<p><em>Gillespie</em> after a phenomenal tank job last season made it to the playoffs. He added Jamyhr Gibbs to bolster a strong, young depth chart along with Deebo Samuel and the Cowboys D to give him one of the deepest rosters. Unfortunately he had a bit of a panic and traded away his future for Ekeler, despite not really needing to. Fortunately his team is still young and affordable and he added Trey McBride to replace the disappointing Kyle Pitts finally. If he doesn\u2019t win this year he is still a contender in the near future.</p>\n<br>\n<p>Way too early 2024/25 prediction: championship contender again</p>\n<br>\n<p>Key FA Acquisitions:</p>\n<br>\n<p>Deebo Samuel - $40</p>\n<br>\n<p>Dallas D/ST - $10</p>\n<br>\n<p>Trey McBride - $0</p>\n<br>\n<p>2023 Trade Chart:</p>\n<br>\n<table class=\"wu-table\">\n<tr><th>Campbell</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Sent</td><td>Received</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Campbell's 2024 First</td><td>Austin Ekeler</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Campbell's 2025 first</td><td>Braden's 2024 Second</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Tony Pollard</td><td></td></tr>\n</table>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">Chasing Dimes</h2>\n<br>\n<p><em>Costello</em> is looking to take advantage of the last year of JaMarr Chase\u2019s rookie contract along with CMC\u2019s relatively affordable contract he assembled a superstar squad. He made aggressive moves through the season to improve at his major weak areas by adding Mike Evans and Lamar Jackson for Jordan Addison and a 2025 first round pick. He will still have CMC for the foreseeable future but his team will look a lot different next season so anything short of a championship will be a disappointment.</p>\n<br>\n<p>Way too early 2024/25 prediction: playoff contender</p>\n<br>\n<p>Key FA Acquisitions:</p>\n<br>\n<p>Saquon Barkley - $90</p>\n<br>\n<p>David Njoku - $0</p>\n<br>\n<p>2023 Trade Chart:</p>\n<br>\n<table class=\"wu-table\">\n<tr><th>Mike</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Sent</td><td>Received</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Mike's 2025 first</td><td>Lamar Jackson</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Brian Robinson</td><td>Mike Evans</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Jakobi Meyers</td><td>Kirk Cousins</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Mike's 2024 second</td><td></td></tr>\n</table>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">There\u2019s a YOU Sheriff In Town</h2>\n<br>\n<p><em>Willen</em> can consider year 1 a major success. Not only did he make the playoffs but he was the top seed! He was aggressive in free agency both in the draft and during the season with Justin Jefferson and Puka Nacua. Unfortunately he made some aggressive, win-now moves that aren\u2019t really helping him win this season. Trading Rachaad White, Drake London and his next two first round picks for Nico Collins and Christian Kirk are looking like panic moves but if he wins this season they will be well worth it. Either way his impact to the league was felt.</p>\n<br>\n<p>Way too early 2024/25 prediction: back to the basement</p>\n<br>\n<p>Key FA Acquisitions:</p>\n<br>\n<p>Justin Jefferson - $95</p>\n<br>\n<p>Puka Nacua - $10</p>\n<br>\n<p>2023 Trade Chart:</p>\n<br>\n<table class=\"wu-table\">\n<tr><th>Bobby</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Sent</td><td>Received</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Bobby's 2024 first</td><td>Nico Collins</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Bobby's 2025 first</td><td>Christian Kirk</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Drake London</td><td>D'onta Foreman</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Bobby's 2nd (Tyjae Spears)</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Bobby's 2024 second</td><td></td></tr>\n</table>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">Patrick Bateman\u2019s</h2>\n<br>\n<p><em>Keaton</em> entered the season with a solid squad with good depth, but he made aggressive moves to get better at RB and another WR taking his team to another level. He could afford to be aggressive since his team will remain affordable for the foreseeable future with Josh Allen and CeeDee Lamb being the most expensive. Both are still reasonable value next season. <em>Keaton</em> appears to have hit another WR in the draft with MSU favorite Jayden Reed giving him more depth into next year.</p>\n<br>\n<p>Way too early 2024/25 prediction: championship contender</p>\n<br>\n<p>Key FA Acquisitions:</p>\n<br>\n<p>CeeDee Lamb - $47</p>\n<br>\n<p>George Kittle - $21</p>\n<br>\n<p>2023 Trade Chart:</p>\n<br>\n<table class=\"wu-table\">\n<tr><th>Blake</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Sent</td><td>Received</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Blake's 2024 first</td><td>Bobby's 2025 first</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Blake's 2025 first</td><td>Rachaad White</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Nico Collins</td><td>Kyren Williams</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Blake's 2024 second</td><td>Cooper Kupp</td></tr>\n</table>\n<br>\n<p class=\"wu-bold\">_______________</p>\n<br>\n<p>**</p>"},{"title":"2023 GPT Trade Evaluation","year":"2023","html":"<p>2023 GPT Trade Evaluation**</p>\n<br>\n<p>Greetings, fellow denizens of the digital arena, and welcome to the Gridiron Algorithmic Gazette! In this binary realm where touchdowns are computed, and quarterbacks are coded, we embark on a quest to dissect the enigmatic world of fantasy football trades with the precision of a thousand lines of code. The whims of fantasy team owners, like unpredictable variables, have led to trade equations that even the most advanced processors might find puzzling. So, fasten your virtual helmets, dear readers, as we unravel the latest gridiron transactions using our trusty bits and bytes in search of the ultimate trade logic \u2013 and perhaps a laugh or two along the way.</p>\n<br>\n<p>Let's evaluate each trade and assign grades to each owner for each trade:</p>\n<br>\n<p class=\"wu-bold\">Trade 1</p>\n<br>\n<li>Mike Receives: Kyle's 2023 First Round Pick (1.5)</li>\n<br>\n<li>Kyle Receives: Brian Robinson</li>\n<br>\n<li>Mike: A</li>\n<br>\n<li>Kyle: C</li>\n<br>\n<li>Winner: Mike</li>\n<br>\n<li>Blurb: Kyle, in a moment of madness, traded a valuable first-round pick for Brian Robinson. Maybe he thought he was getting a Brian Urlacher. Oops!</li>\n<br>\n<p class=\"wu-bold\">Trade 2</p>\n<br>\n<li>Braden Receives: Bobby's 2023 second round pick (2.1) and Bobby's 2024 second round pick</li>\n<br>\n<li>Bobby Receives: Rachaad White</li>\n<br>\n<li>Braden: A</li>\n<br>\n<li>Bobby: C</li>\n<br>\n<li>Winner: Braden</li>\n<br>\n<li>Blurb: Bobby decided to bet the future on White, but it looks like he's writing himself a \"White\" check for disappointment.</li>\n<br>\n<p class=\"wu-bold\">Trade 3</p>\n<br>\n<li>Braden Receives: Marquise Brown</li>\n<br>\n<li>Collin Receives: The later of Braden/Bobby's 2024 second round pick</li>\n<br>\n<li>Braden: B</li>\n<br>\n<li>Collin: C</li>\n<br>\n<li>Winner: Braden</li>\n<br>\n<li>Blurb: Collin chose to wait for the future, but Braden got Marquise Brown. Maybe Collin is practicing patience, or maybe he's just \"Brown\"-nosing the wrong deals.</li>\n<br>\n<p class=\"wu-bold\">Trade 4</p>\n<br>\n<li>Braden Receives: New England New England Patriots and Mike's 2024 second round pick</li>\n<br>\n<li>Mike Receives: Tank Bigsby</li>\n<br>\n<li>Braden: B</li>\n<br>\n<li>Mike: B</li>\n<br>\n<li>Winner: Draw</li>\n<br>\n<li>Blurb: Braden tried to shore up his defense, but Mike took a gamble on a \"Tank.\" Let's hope it doesn't run out of gas!</li>\n<br>\n<p class=\"wu-bold\">Trade 5</p>\n<br>\n<li>Adam/Matt Receives: James Conner</li>\n<br>\n<li>Kyle Receives: Adam/Matt's 2024 First Round pick</li>\n<br>\n<li>Adam/Matt: B</li>\n<br>\n<li>Kyle: B</li>\n<br>\n<li>Winner: Draw</li>\n<br>\n<li>Blurb: Kyle was so excited about getting a first-round pick that he forgot James Conner is a scoring machine. A classic case of getting \"Connered\" into a deal.</li>\n<br>\n<p class=\"wu-bold\">Trade 6</p>\n<br>\n<li>Blake Receives: Cooper Kupp</li>\n<br>\n<li>Kyle Receives: Rashod Bateman and Blake's 2024 First Round Pick</li>\n<br>\n<li>Blake: A</li>\n<br>\n<li>Kyle: B</li>\n<br>\n<li>Winner: Blake</li>\n<br>\n<li>Blurb: Kyle traded Cooper Kupp for Bateman and a future pick. It's like trading a gourmet meal for a recipe and some ingredients!</li>\n<br>\n<p class=\"wu-bold\">Trade 7</p>\n<br>\n<li>Kyle Receives: Justin Tucker</li>\n<br>\n<li>Brad Receives: Dalvin Cook and Evan McPherson</li>\n<br>\n<li>Kyle: A</li>\n<br>\n<li>Brad: C</li>\n<br>\n<li>Winner: Kyle</li>\n<br>\n<li>Blurb: Brad got a Cook, but Kyle secured the most accurate kicker in NFL history. That's a \"kick\"-in-the-pants move!</li>\n<br>\n<p class=\"wu-bold\">Trade 8</p>\n<br>\n<li>Collin Receives: Raheem Mostert</li>\n<br>\n<li>Casey Receives: Collins' 2024 First Round and Second Round picks</li>\n<br>\n<li>Collin: B</li>\n<br>\n<li>Casey: B</li>\n<br>\n<li>Winner: Draw</li>\n<br>\n<li>Blurb: Casey just couldn't resist those shiny future picks. Collin, on the other hand, hopes Mostert is the \"mostert\" of RBs.</li>\n<br>\n<p class=\"wu-bold\">Trade 9</p>\n<br>\n<li>Casey Receives: Jordan Addison</li>\n<br>\n<li>Mike Receives: Mike Evans</li>\n<br>\n<li>Casey: B</li>\n<br>\n<li>Mike: A</li>\n<br>\n<li>Winner: Mike</li>\n<br>\n<li>Blurb: Casey got Jordan Addison, but Mike got the \"real\" Mike. Addison might have a bright future, but Mike has proven himself as a fantasy stud.</li>\n<br>\n<p class=\"wu-bold\">Trade 10</p>\n<br>\n<li>Braden Receives: Kyle's 2024 Second Round Pick</li>\n<br>\n<li>Kyle Receives: Antonio Gibson</li>\n<br>\n<li>Braden: A</li>\n<br>\n<li>Kyle: A</li>\n<br>\n<li>Winner: Draw</li>\n<br>\n<li>Blurb: Braden invested in the future, but Kyle ran off with the present in the form of Antonio Gibson.</li>\n<br>\n<p class=\"wu-bold\">Trade 11</p>\n<br>\n<li>Blake Receives: Rachaad White and Bobby's 2025 first round pick</li>\n<br>\n<li>Bobby Receives: Nico Collins</li>\n<br>\n<li>Blake: A</li>\n<br>\n<li>Bobby: C</li>\n<br>\n<li>Winner: Blake</li>\n<br>\n<li>Blurb: Bobby gave up the future and got a Nico Collins. Meanwhile, Blake secured another White whale of a deal.</li>\n<br>\n<p class=\"wu-bold\">Trade 12</p>\n<br>\n<li>Kyle Receives: Jakobi Meyers</li>\n<br>\n<li>Mike Receives: Kirk Cousins</li>\n<br>\n<li>Kyle: B</li>\n<br>\n<li>Mike: B</li>\n<br>\n<li>Winner: Draw</li>\n<br>\n<li>Blurb: In the battle of Meyers vs. Cousins, Kyle won this round. Mike now has a \"Cousin\"ly QB duo.</li>\n<br>\n<p class=\"wu-bold\">Trade 13</p>\n<br>\n<li>Jeff Receives: Cam Akers and Mikes 2024 Second round pick</li>\n<br>\n<li>Braden Receives: Darren Waller</li>\n<br>\n<li>Jeff: A</li>\n<br>\n<li>Braden: B</li>\n<br>\n<li>Winner: Jeff</li>\n<br>\n<li>Blurb: Jeff is \"Akers\"-tually thrilled with this trade, but Braden's Waller is safe from another season-ending injury.</li>\n<br>\n<p class=\"wu-bold\">Trade 14</p>\n<br>\n<li>Braden Receives: Austin Ekeler and Jalin Hyatt</li>\n<br>\n<li>Adam/Matt Receives: Rhamondre Stevenson, Terry McLaurin, Braden's 2024 and 2025 first round picks</li>\n<br>\n<li>Braden: A</li>\n<br>\n<li>Adam/Matt: C</li>\n<br>\n<li>Winner: Braden</li>\n<br>\n<li>Blurb: Adam/Matt gave away a king's ransom for a running back named Stevenson. Braden, however, is living the \"Ekeler\" life.</li>\n<br>\n<p class=\"wu-bold\">Trade 15</p>\n<br>\n<li>Brad Receives: David Montgomery</li>\n<br>\n<li>Adam/Matt Receives: George Pickens and picks based on the following conditions.</li>\n<br>\n<li>Brad: C</li>\n<br>\n<li>Adam/Matt: A</li>\n<br>\n<li>Winner: Adam/Matt</li>\n<br>\n<li>Blurb: Adam/Matt played the \"conditional pick\" card, leaving Brad feeling like he's trading with a fortune teller. Will Montgomery's health determine the trade's outcome?</li>\n<br>\n<p>Ranking based on potential impact on the league:</p>\n<br>\n<p>1. Trade 7 (Kyle got Justin Tucker)</p>\n<br>\n<p>2. Trade 6 (Blake got Cooper Kupp)</p>\n<br>\n<p>3. Trade 14 (Braden got Austin Ekeler)</p>\n<br>\n<p>4. Trade 3 (Braden got Marquise Brown)</p>\n<br>\n<p>5. Trade 11 (Blake got Rachaad White and a 2025 first-round pick)</p>\n<br>\n<p>6. Trade 10 (Braden got Kyle's 2024 second-round pick)</p>\n<br>\n<p>7. Trade 5 (Adam/Matt got James Conner)</p>\n<br>\n<p>8. Trade 2 (Braden got Bobby's 2023 and 2024 second-round picks)</p>\n<br>\n<p>9. Trade 8 (Collin got Raheem Mostert)</p>\n<br>\n<p>10. Trade 9 (Mike got Mike Evans)</p>\n<br>\n<p>11. Trade 12 (Kyle got Jakobi Meyers)</p>\n<br>\n<p>12. Trade 1 (Mike got Kyle's 2023 first-round pick)</p>\n<br>\n<p>13. Trade 4 (Draw, Braden got New England D/ST and a 2024 second-round pick, Mike got Tank Bigsby)</p>\n<br>\n<p>14. Trade 15 (Draw, Brad got David Montgomery, Adam/Matt got George Pickens and conditional picks)</p>\n<br>\n<p>15. Trade 13 (Draw, Jeff got Cam Akers and a 2024 second-round pick, Braden</p>\n<br>\n<p>got Darren Waller)</p>\n<br>\n<p>As we conclude our examination of these intriguing fantasy football trades, it's important to remember that in this unpredictable world, one never quite knows what the future holds. While some owners might be strategizing their way to championship glory, others, like our good-natured friend Jeff, might still be seeking that elusive playoff spot. But fear not, for every season brings new opportunities, and perhaps one day, Jeff will find the playbook that leads him to the promised land of the playoffs. Until then, let's continue to enjoy the whims and quirks of fantasy football, where even the unlikeliest underdogs can become the champions of the gridiron. After all, in fantasy football, as in life, surprises abound, and every season carries the potential for a legendary comeback.</p>\n<br>\n<p>**</p>"},{"title":"2023 Week 4","year":"2023","html":"<p>2023 Week 4: **The Leaves are Changing</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">What Can Brown Do For You?</h2>\n<br>\n<p>The story this far is largely the same for <em>White</em>\u2026 It\u2019s been 84 years since he\u2019s lost a game. However this year just seems (feels? hopes to be?) different. He\u2019s only had one true blow up game in four weeks and has a wild 67.3 to 59.28 win in week 1. <em>White</em> still has an elite core in Hurts, Henry, Brown, and Adams, so he\u2019s going to compete every week. The issue is he\u2019s yet to find a reliable second RB and flex to this point in the season. He\u2019s still dangerous if/when he finds trustable options, but if any of his studs (especially Hurts) gets\u2026 hurt\u2026 then he might end up actually losing a game.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">There\u2019s a YOU Sheriff In Town</h2>\n<br>\n<p>Yes, yes there is. <em>Willen</em> has made a quick turnaround of the team formerly known as Blake Bortles Wikipedia Club. He was aggressive during the draft and had strong pre-season hype all-things-considered. He kept his aggression going into the season and landed the prized free agent target week 1 in Nacua who has been the best rookie performer so far to add to the top pick in the rookie draft in Bijan. This team is built to win this season, but depth at WR may be an issue if Kupp steals his role back in LA or Jefferson misses any time.</p>\n<br>\n<p class=\"wu-bold\">Puka Nacua managers seeing Cooper Kupp coming off IR</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">Patrick Bateman\u2019s</h2>\n<br>\n<p><em>Keater</em> is feeling himself after a big week, so much that he requested this write-up. His 147.1 week 4 score makes it clear why. However when you dig into the numbers a little more, his points for is just the 8th best even with the explosion last week and that\u2019s without suffering any injuries. The brightside is he has Kupp coming back in the near future, the downside is WR is a clear strength for him already. Allen is clearly the top QB and Pacheco/Mattison are solid enough to keep him competitive every week. If either of his RBs get hurt or Mattison loses his job, then <em>Keater</em> - like many - will be struggling to find RBs. He has the inside track to win the division and will look to secure a bye to make the playoffs a little easier.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">Drunkin Polaks</h2>\n<br>\n<p>We\u2019re playing fantasy football, <em>Chorazyczewski</em> is playing chess. He\u2019s made eight trades since last season, three more than the next highest person. There\u2019s a world where <em>Chorazyczewski</em> is 4-0 with Kupp joining McCaffery to take his team to another level. It\u2019s rare to see a rebuild from a competitor, but he\u2019s addicted to trading. With that being said, his strategy is clear (despite being 3-1) and he has been slowly acquiring solid young players on very cheap deals to go along with his four 2024 first round picks. Swift and Robinson are relatively cheap, productive players. Rice has shown flashes of talent as have Stroud and Dell who he got off waivers. Look for <em>Chorazyczewski</em> to continue to compile assets</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">Windy City Waterboys</h2>\n<br>\n<p>Going into the season with much hype, <em>Gillespie</em> has largely lived up to it despite a 2-2 start. He\u2019s leading the league in points for and has depth across the board. The flipside of the coin is he\u2019s relying heavily on Dallas Defense has four touchdowns in as many weeks, which is unsustainable despite them being the clear best defense in the NFL right now. <em>Gillespie</em> is likely to make the playoffs and should have success navigating the long season of byes and injuries giving him a chance at the coveted playoff bye week. The only question remains is can <em>Gillespie</em> handle the hype train?</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">Show Me Your TDs</h2>\n<br>\n<p>After a huge week 1, <em>Geraldo</em> struggled to fill the void of Jonthan Taylor and Aaron Jones. Now with those two back and the emergence of Achane (thanks Lin), RB is back to being a position of strength. He still has his two top five WRs and strong depth beyond that at the position. Despite Fields' underperformance, the only glaring position of concern for <em>Geraldo</em> is at TE with a rolling cast of characters, starting four different guys in five weeks. He is once again a contender, sitting at second in points for and his roster appears to be hitting its stride.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">The Mixon Administration</h2>\n<br>\n<p>It hasn\u2019t been easy up to this point with injuries and some underperformance, but <em>Laethem</em> was able to Shake It Off and now sits at 2-2. He backed into Ford who will be filling in the Blank Space left by the shocking Chubb injury. Allen is performing better than <em>Laethem</em>\u2019s Wildest Dreams when he drafted him and Mahomes continues his Love Story with Kelce. His depth isn\u2019t great but having the top guys at the onesie positions will ensure he competes every week. <em>Laethem</em> will look to make it Back to December with playoff hopes, despite being on the outside looking in at this point of the season.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">Lets Cook</h2>\n<br>\n<p><em>Steinmetz </em>and <em>Banchiu </em>have been hit by the injury bug with Andrews missing week 1, Ekeler being out since week 2, Montgomery missing a week, and Diontae Johnson going on IR. Despite all of that they are at 2-2 and should have Ekleler back after his bye week fully healthy. They were aggressive to obtain Conner for their 2024 first, which is good value for a starting RB and improved their depth. Their big weakness is at WR, even when Johnson comes back. DK has been solid, but not elite, and Doubs has shown some flashes in the absence of Watson. Hopkins has been a major disappointment and Hyatt isn\u2019t ready for the big time yet. Their Jackson/Andrews stack is the poor-man's version of Laethems stud pairing, but it is definitely a strong duo compared to most.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">Chasing Dimes</h2>\n<br>\n<p><em>Costello</em>\u2019s studs and duds approach has been largely up and down as predicted. Jacobs and Chase have looked more like duds until recently and Barkley has been out for a few weeks. A crafty, win-now trade of Addison for Evans will help give him some much needed support at WR, but QB remains an area of concern as does RBs below his top guys. Despite a 2-2 start, <em>Costello looks</em> to still have a strong team at the top and some decent options to fill in the gaps. I expect him to compete for the division and make the playoffs, and if healthy should make a deep run. Much like the 49ers, CMC will continue to carry his squad.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">The Benchwarmers</h2>\n<br>\n<p><em>Smith</em> saw the draft was in Detroit in 2024 and figured he\u2019ll already be there anyways might as well tank. This is without a doubt the worst roster the league has ever seen this early in the season, but on the positive side he flipped Moestert for a top three 2024 first round, a 2024 second round pick, and Evans for Jordan Addison. He knows he\u2019s in rebuild mode and he has intelligently deconstructed his team to infuse young talent and assets. Richardson looks like he will be the next great rushing QB, Addison is explosive at WR, and Kincaid is solid for a rookie TE. <em>Smith</em> will be whispering to himself on Sundays - We\u2019ll get \u2018em next year boys.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">Burrow Mckokiner</h2>\n<br>\n<p>This is why you don\u2019t go all in with a 7-2 offsuit hand\u2026 7-2 offsuit is probably an unfair comparison for <em>Frink</em>\u2019s team as it\u2019s not THAT bad. Dropping his 2023 first round pick in Achane and trading his 2024 first round pick (right now second overall) for Moestert right before the former took the latter\u2019s job is not ideal. Kyren Williams was a strong free agent add and his WRs are some of the best in the league, especially Flowers and Mims. His team is better than his record suggests, but until Burrow gets back to his normal self it\u2019ll be tough to compete consistently.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">Breece\u2019s Pierces</h2>\n<br>\n<p>Does <em>Butler</em> go with the homer approach and take Blake Corum? Could he go against his roots and draft Marvin Harrison Jr.? Maybe he takes a different route and goes with the stud QB in Caleb Williams? It\u2019s a shame we\u2019re here again with <em>Butler</em>. There was some hope for his season this year then high priced free agents Rodgers and Chubb went out for the year. His top two rookie picks, Johnston and JSN, have struggled early in the year, but LaPorta has looked great.</p>\n<br>\n<p>**</p>"},{"title":"2023 Season Preview","year":"2023","html":"<p>2023 Season Preview: **A YOU era of competition has arrived</p>\n<br>\n<p>Welcome back to another year of football! We\u2019ve been waiting all year to fire up the trash talk. We can now suffer through painful losses and celebrate big wins. There have already been big and small wins and losses during the pre-season with great values or over-zealous drafting. This appears to be the year where champions turn to pumpkins and perennial losers (<em>Butler</em>) hodl the line to turn their fortunes around. Then again, we thought that last year and the normal names were in the playoffs again. We will find out much more over the next 14 weeks of football!</p>\n<br>\n<p>NAV = sum of players actual value less the Fantasy Pros values summed by team</p>\n<br>\n<p>Team Review = average of a teams position group rankings</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">There\u2019s a YOU Sheriff in Town **Bobby Willen</h2>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Draft Strategy: </strong>Get a stud and as many Bears as possible</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Value rating: </strong>Solid Value (21 NAV)</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Team Review:</strong> 6/12 - Playoff contender with a long future runway</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>QB: </strong>11/12 - Dak is a low end QB1 and is overvalued for his skill level. He won\u2019t kill <em>Willen</em>, but he isn\u2019t going to be carrying him to wins.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>RB: </strong>2/12 - Bijan might be the best prospect since Barkley and Ettienne proved to be solid last season. Both are affordable for this year and Bijan is obviously a keeper for the next 3 seasons. White is young and on a decent deal if he performs as a RB2/Flex and Cook is an interesting prospect who should step into a much better role in a good offense.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>WR:</strong> 7/12 - Despite blowing his wad on the top WR in the league in Jefferson, he struggles with depth beyond London and even London isn\u2019t that special. Homer pick Mooney is a good value and the ultra talented Boyd makes his way back to the Sheriff. There is keeper value here too, but there will need to be some better production from Jeudy and London this season.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>TE: </strong>3/12 - Hockenson is a solid player and might be the second target on the Vikings. He ranks ahead of Kittle because of his value and age make him good enough to keep for a couple years.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Flex: </strong>7/12 - <em>Willen</em> has a few options in the aforementioned White, Mooney, Jeudy, and Cook. The issue is there isn\u2019t a truly reliable option at this point in the season.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">Windy City Boys** *Campbell Gillespie</h2>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Draft Strategy: </strong>Save Dat Money</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Value rating: </strong>Insane Value!!! (102 NAV)</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Team Review:</strong> 4.2/12 - Championship contender with dynasty potential</p>\n<br>\n<p>(Editor\u2019s Note: I can\u2019t believe I wrote that)</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>QB: </strong>7/12 - Herbert is a solid starter who should have a bounce back season. The only reason Herbert isn\u2019t higher is he\u2019s going for market value.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>RB: </strong>3/12 - Pollard isn\u2019t exactly a superstar yet, but he has a chance to reach that tier with the backfield to himself this season. Beyond Pollard, <em>Gillespie</em> has strong depth but not a clear cut number 2 with some shared backfield that need to be worked out as the season goes. Gibbs has huge potential now and going forward to pair with Walker.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>WR: </strong>4/12 - Waddle and St. Brown are top WR2s that are on the cusp of WR1s. Adding in Deebo and Pittman gives him much strong bye-week/injury depth with upside if the starters don\u2019t perform.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>TE: </strong>7/12 - Two solid TE1s in Freiermuth and Pitts, but neither is a stud yet. They are young and each has upside so they balance each other out well.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Flex: </strong>2/12 - Deebo, Pittman, Gibbs, and Harris are all great options at flex, his bench is deep.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">Patrick Bateman\u2019s **Blake Keaton</h2>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Draft Strategy: </strong>Spend up for talent</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Value rating:</strong> Solid Value (26 NAV)</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Team Review:</strong> 6.6/12 - Playoff contender with a narrow window</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>QB: </strong>2/12 - Josh Allen is basically super man. He was hurt last year and still was a top level QB. <em>Keaton</em> is still getting great value for Allen.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>RB: </strong>12/12 - Mattison and Pacheco are both starters, but neither is exciting. His depth is a problem with a bunch of secondary RBs on their own team between Allgeier, Wilson, and Warren.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>WR: </strong>3/12 - Ceedee and Wilson are a solid starting pair. He has a lot of potential with Bateman and Reed along with solid fill-in guys in Davis and Smith-Schuster that he did slightly overpay for. Wilson is potentially going to be unlocked with Rodgers for at least this season and maybe next season too.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>TE: </strong>4/12 - Kittle is a strong starter who was a slight overpay but definitely improves his starting lineup as long as he\u2019s healthy.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Flex: </strong>11/12 - Mid options at flex, but he at least has a lot of them\u2026</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">Burrow Mckokiner** *Collin Frink</h2>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Draft Strategy: </strong>Secure Burrow and fill roster</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Value rating: </strong>Overvalued (-40 NAV)</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Team Review:</strong> 8/12 - Maybe playoffs, maybe Marvin Harrison Jr\u2026 Who knows?</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>QB: </strong>5/12 - Burrow is a solid value and will help propel <em>Frink</em> to some wins. He will be a solid value for the foreseeable future.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>RB: </strong>11/12 - Sanders was strong last year but in a much worse offense this year. He is slightly overvalued, but worth it. Gibson is a high upside player, but early in the season will be a challenge. Mitchell and Achane also provide some potential upside if things break their way.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>WR: </strong>6/12 - Smith and Cooper are both solid starters. Neither is a super-stud but they are a strong starting pair. Aiyuk was a slight overpay, but Williams and Smith were strong values. Beyond that Flowers, Williams, and Mims are great values with league winning future upside.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>TE: </strong>12/12 - Fant is unproven, but in a strong offense so he might have some potential.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Flex: </strong>6/12 - The WR depth helps a lot here with 5 different potential options. <em>Frink </em>will be struggling every week to find the right play unless someone gets hot during the season.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">Drunkin Polaks** Kyle Chorazyczewski</h2>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Draft Strategy: </strong>Spend up for upside</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Value rating: </strong>Overvalued (-36 NAV)</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Team Review:</strong> 6.8/12 - Playoff contender this year</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>QB: </strong>8/12 - Watson is an overspend right now, but if he get back to his pre-massage version then <em>Chorazyczewski</em> is in for a happy ending.</p>\n<br>\n<br>\n<br>\n<p><strong>RB: </strong>8/12 - Cook and Swift were both overspends that have potential to turn <em>Chorazyczewski</em> into a championship contender or be out of the league by the end of the season. Robinson and Connor are safe buy-week fill-ins at affordable prices.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>WR: </strong>5/12 - Kupp is a super stud if he and Stafford are healthy, but that is a big assumption as both of them get a year older and are coming off of injuries. Higgins is a solid number 2 even if <em>Chorazyczewski</em> over paid to keep him, it was probably worth it. Unfortunately his depth is shaky with an unproven, constantly injured guy with potential in Toney and a possibly past his prime Sutton.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>TE: </strong>9/12 - Schultz is a target hog now in an offense without a true number 1 receiver. He should be solid, but unspectacular this season.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Flex: </strong>4/12 - Cook/Swift/Connor/Robinson are not great starting RBs, but the depth provides <em>Chorazyczewski</em> a lot of strong flex options.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">Breece\u2019s Pierces** Jeffrey Butler</h2>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Draft Strategy: </strong>Add a top player in every position</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Value rating:</strong> Slight Value (10 NAV)</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Team Review:</strong> 7.2/12 - Playoff contender with potential to become perennial playoff lock</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>QB: </strong>10/12 - Rodgers is coming off a rough season with Green Bay, if he bounces back this could be a solid move but if he\u2019s washed that will cost <em>Butler</em> his best shot at a playoff berth this year. If Murray plays this season that could add a potential cheat code for late in the season.\u2019</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>RB: </strong>5/12 - Securing Chubb, regardless of the high payment, gives <em>Butler</em> the strong starter he needed. Pierce is solid, but not amazing. Unfortunately for <em>Butler, </em>everything is trending for Hall to have a slow start to the season and likely a limited ceiling year with the Jets bringing in Cook.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>WR: </strong>9/12 - Depth. His WR pool is deep and has lots of potential with three of the best second year receivers (Olave, Watson, Dotson) which allowed <em>Butler</em> to overspend on other positions. Moore finds himself in a better QB situation so he might be worth the money if he gets fully unlocked. JSN and Johnston give <em>Butler</em> a pipeline for future success as well. Only reason it\u2019s not higher is he doesn\u2019t have the true top tier guy, but that could change quickly.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>TE: </strong>7/12 - Waller is a decent value this year, especially if he is the top receiving option for the Giants. The worry is that he gets hurt again or the passing offense is as limited as it was last year. Laporta offers <em>Butler</em> a potential superstar TE at a low price that would round out his team nicely if he hits.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Flex: </strong>5/12 - The key to <em>Butler</em>\u2019s flex is his depth at WR. Watson and Dotson offer big play ability that will give his team boom scoring in any given week.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">The Mixon Administration ***James Laethem </h2>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Draft Strategy: </strong>Get a receiver</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Value rating: </strong>Neutral (5 NAV)</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Team Review:</strong> 5.8/12 - Playoff contender this season, with championship potential</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>QB: </strong>1/12 - Mahomes is the GOAT and even at $22 is a discount.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>RB: </strong>7/12 - Mixon is a low-end RB1 who\u2019s a little overpriced for how he\u2019s been performing. Javonte is coming off an ACL injury and Jamaal is in a crowded backfield when Kamara gets back. <em>Laethem</em> has solid, but not spectacular RB play. He has some high upside handcuffs on the bench though.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>WR: </strong>11/12 - Ridley hasn\u2019t played in two seasons and Allen is getting up there in age, but if they can keep their peak performance then <em>Laethem</em>\u2019s rank would be much higher than this. His depth is also limited unless Burks takes a big leap.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>TE: </strong>1/12 - Kelce, also the GOAT at his position, is worth every penny and should have another big year. The only question is how much longer will he be dominant?</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Flex: </strong>9/12 - Lack of depth makes <em>Laethem</em>\u2019s flex potential much weaker than his peers.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">Show Me Your TDs** *Braden Geraldo </h2>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Draft Strategy: </strong>Overspend on players who won\u2019t play</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Value rating: </strong>Strong Value (39 NAV)</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Team Review:</strong> 4/12 - Championship contender this year and maybe one more year</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>QB: </strong>4/12 - Fields is still not a proven consistent stud, but he\u2019s very affordable and has the potential to lead the position in scoring.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>RB: </strong>4/12 - <em>Geraldo</em> has two solid low-end RB1s. If/when Taylor gets back his rank seems too low and would be second or third. Akers and Penny both have the potential to be RB1s if they get the opportunity and stay healthy.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>WR: </strong>1/12 - Hill and Diggs both have the role and talent to lead the position in scoring and are near locks to finish as top level WR1s if they stay healthy. They are both good values, but getting older. <em>Geraldo</em> took some shots on talent in Mclaurin and Thomas, but Mclaurin is injured and Thomas probably will be soon. Fortunately, Mclaurin should be healthy by the time bye weeks role around and Hollywood is going to be a strong bye week fill in.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>TE: </strong>8/12 - This is the shakiest position he has, but Njoku is going to be involved in what could be a better offense. He\u2019s not going to break the position but he\u2019ll at least be a low-end starting TE. Chig is an intriguing second year player who was highly efficient last year.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Flex: </strong>3/12 - RB and WR depth are strong which gives him a lot of options at flex. This is another case of many options leading to wrong choices, but again if Taylor is back or Akers/Penny live up to their potential then only a few teams would have stronger flexes.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">Me and Ma Homies** *Casey Smith</h2>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Draft Strategy: </strong>Keep Dobbins at ALL COSTS</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Value rating: </strong>Insanely Overvalued (-86 NAV)</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Team Review:</strong> 10.4/12 - Marvin Harrison Jr.\u2019s future team</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>QB: </strong>9/12 - Tua is one of the worst starters in the league especially at his cost, but Richardson could be a stud on a cheap contract which is driving up his ranking.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>RB: </strong>9/12 - Starting Dobbins and Mckinnon week one is not ideal despite Mckinnon\u2019s late season heroics and Dobbins\u2019 potential. When Kamara returns, <em>Smith</em> will have a stronger stable but his depth is shaky and Kamara isn\u2019t as dominant as he used to be.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>WR: </strong>12/12 - <em>Smith</em> has four secondary WRs that he\u2019ll choose from each week. Lockett and Kirk are solid, but both have a new contender in town looking to take their targets. Evans went from Brady to Baker, which can\u2019t be good for his outlook. Cooks is not a spring chicken any more, but maybe he can have a late career renaissance. Depth isn\u2019t bad, but upside is limited.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>TE: </strong>11/12 - Dulcich showed some promise last year, which is why he is slightly higher than Fant. However there is a big gap between the 10 and 11 rankings here.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Flex: </strong>11/12 - This goes back to his lack of depth at RB, but he has some interesting WRs to plug into his flex. He just doesn\u2019t have much pop.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">Ch-ubb-y Chaser **Michael Costello</h2>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Draft Strategy: </strong>Spend all on one stud</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Value rating: </strong>Neutral (2 NAV)</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Team Review:</strong> 6.4/12 - near playoff lock, but needs to figure out a lot of things</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>QB: </strong>12/12 - Some mix between Dangeruss and Danny Dimes is an ugly plan into the season. <em>Costello</em> will likely be shuffling guys off the waivers trying to find some magic throughout the season.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>RB: </strong>1/12 - It\u2019s not even close from one to two in these rankings. His team value looks worse than it should based on the data because of the insane RB core he\u2019s put together. <em>Costello</em> has three of the top six running backs. Outside of the top three, his depth is non-existent, so injuries and byes will cause a lot of issues.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>WR: </strong>8/12 - Chase is a solid top 10, maybe even top five talent but he can\u2019t make up for the other WRs on this team. Meyers and Moore are barely worthy of roster spots on most of the teams in the league. Addison is an intriguing prospect, but it\u2019s doubtful he will be consistent enough to be a weekly starter.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>TE: </strong>10/12 - Higbee is more valuable on <em>Costello</em>\u2019s squad since he is likely to be targeted a decent amount, but he has no chance of a top end season. This is another position where a constant carousel of players will be flowing through his team.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Flex: </strong>1/12 - See RB above\u2026 His flex would be most teams top RB, but any injuries creates an issue.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">Let\u2019s Cook** *Adam Banchiu* & *Matthew Steinmetz</h2>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Draft Strategy: </strong>Fill in the gaps</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Value rating:</strong> Slightly Overvalued (-19 NAV)</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Team Review:</strong> 6.8/12 - playoff contender this year</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>QB: </strong>6/12 - Lamar is better than the sixth best QB when healthy, but health has been rare for him in recent years. The value is still fair here, but definitely not a strong value. Goff provides <em>Banchiu</em> and <em>Steinmetz</em> some stability in case injuries hit again.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>RB: </strong>6/12 - Ekeler is still a stud and should finish as a top tier RB. Montgomery is a solid second RB, but might not even be the best RB on his team. After that he has a couple of rookies who might get some work this year, but can\u2019t be trusted to play at this point.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>WR: </strong>10/12 - <em>Banchiu</em> and <em>Steinmetz </em>have three solid WRs with talent, but none of them is likely to be a top end WR any time soon. Metcalf has a crowded WR core, Hopkins is getting older and on his third team in four years, and Diontae hasn\u2019t scored a TD in two seasons. Doubs is an interesting young prospect and Hyatt could be decent but neither is likely to be a starter this year.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>TE: </strong>2/12 - Andrews is still a top level option despite a down season last year. Engram is the best TE2 out of any team and would help some other teams out. It wouldn\u2019t be a bad idea to trade Engram for a usable RB or WR.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Flex: </strong>10/12 - depth being the main issue here, <em>Banchiu</em> and <em>Steinmetz</em> have a solid flex to start the season but when byes and injuries hit they are going to be scraping the barrel.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">Derr-lick My Henry Balls** *Bradley White</h2>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Draft Strategy: </strong>try to get a RB that\u2019s usable</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Value rating:</strong> Neutral (6 NAV)</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Team Review:</strong> 5.8/12 - Playoff lock and championship contender once again</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>QB: </strong>3/12 - Hurts is not Allen or Mahomes but he was great last year and returns with largely the same offensive skill group. He\u2019s still a great value and Pickett is a strong back up at good value.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>RB: </strong>10/12 - this is where things get interesting for <em>White</em>\u2026 Henry is still a strong starter which is why this ranking isn\u2019t worse, but he\u2019s quickly approaching 30 and has had a lot of usage over the past few years. Behind Henry he has slightly more than nothing. Foreman, his presumed week one starter, might finish the season fourth on his team in rushing. Zeke, Gainwell, and Jackson are unlikely to lead their teams in anything also.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>WR: </strong>2/12 - Adams took a slight step back with Carr gone and Jimmy G in, but he\u2019s still one of the best in the league. AJ Brown is a strong number two and would be one on many teams. Godwin is a decent third option, but his offense could be ugly with Baker. Pickens offers decent value with potential to carry the torch going forward for <em>White</em>.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>TE: </strong>6/12 - Goedert is fair value and should be consistent again. He is one of the last of the trustable TEs providing <em>White</em> a slight advantage to the teams without TEs.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Flex: </strong>8/12 - Godwin and Pickens are decent options, but he isn\u2019t going to find any help from the RB group at flex.</p>"},{"title":"2022 Week 14","year":"2022","html":"<p>2022 Week 14: Playoff Preview</p>\n<br>\n<p>It\u2019s the final week of the season and five teams are locked into a playoff spot, with one spot remaining. Here are some situations to watch for this weekend in the race for the CAS.</p>\n<br>\n<p>The one seed is Derr-lick My Henry Balls (<em>White</em>), which he all but locked up with a big week 12 win against rival Show Me Your TDs (<em>Geraldo</em>). <em>White</em> will be looking to add to his winnings by out-scoring <em>Geraldo</em> by 13.52 points this week. If he can do so he would get $120 total heading into the playoffs, if not he will take $40 for the division. <em>Geraldo</em> on the other hand has locked in either the fourth or fifth seed and will play Me and Ma Homies (<em>Smith</em>) regardless of their matchup this week. <em>Smith</em> should definitely make a name change to give himself some playoff juice a la <em>Frink</em>\u2019s Burrow Mckokiner.</p>\n<br>\n<p>The other big thing to watch up at the top is who will lock in the other bye between Chubby Chaser (<em>Costello</em>) and defending champions Let\u2019s Cook (<em>Steinmetz</em> and <em>Banchiu</em>). After a narrow victory thanks to a couple wild plays over Breece\u2019s Pierces (<em>Butler</em>), <em>Costello</em> kept his path to a first round bye alive. <em>Banchiu</em> and <em>Steinmetz</em> win against a solid Patrick Bateman\u2019s (<em>Keaton</em>) team and they get the second seed, but if they lose and <em>Costello </em>wins they will play the six seed next week. It is more important now that Lamar Jackson is likely out a couple of weeks. Both have locked up their respective divisions, so either way they net $40.</p>\n<br>\n<p>The main event for the weekend is the battle for the final seed. <em>Butler</em> finds himself in a win and in situation, which would be his first ever playoff appearance in league history in case you didn\u2019t know. He has an uphill battle after The Mixon Administration (<em>Laethem</em>) dropped Derek Carr in a PSYOP to tank <em>Butler</em>\u2019s playoff hopes. Should <em>Butler</em> lose he would need both <em>Laethem</em> and Drunkin Polaks (<em>Chorazyczewski</em>) to lose also in order to still make it. <em>Laethem</em>\u2019s matchup with There\u2019s a YOU Sheriff in Town (<em>Willen</em>) makes that scenario highly unlikely. <em>Chorazyczewski</em> still has some hope to make the playoffs, even if <em>Laethem</em> wins, if he can win and score 8.12 more points than <em>Laethem</em>. His matchup against <em>Costello </em>is, unfortunately for him, the most difficult of the three, but crazier things have happened!</p>\n<br>\n<p>Other things to note\u2026</p>\n<br>\n<p>The top pick has been locked up by <em>Willen</em>! Will he pick Bijan Robinson, the obvious pick as of today, or will a surprise NFL draft day move or combine performance change his mind\u2026 CEH over JT 2.0?</p>\n<br>\n<p><em>Gillespie</em> has all but locked up the second pick unless <em>Frink </em>loses and scores 66.84 fewer points, which is highly unlikely. This appears to be a deep class, but there is no clear cut number two player so look for <em>Gillespie</em> to make a road trip to Indianapolis this spring to do some scouting.</p>\n<br>\n<p>The battle for the third and fourth pick is also alive between <em>Keaton</em> and <em>Frink</em>, with the edge going to <em>Frink</em> should both experience the same outcome this week. Unfortunately for <em>Keaton</em>, he went \u201call-in\u201d trading his first round pick for Deebo Samuel (lol) to <em>Butler</em>. This provides a potential silver lining for <em>Butler</em> losing to <em>Frink</em> as it may improve his second draft pick.</p>\n<br>\n<p>Overall it should be a fun last week of the regular season with a lot of score watching by everyone. Playoffs should get exciting as there are three teams and potentially four that have a real shot this year. For the teams that don\u2019t make the playoffs, remember that this is your last week to pick up players and the winner of the consolation bracket gets $10 extra to their post draft FAAB budget to help you capitalize on people dropping young rookie running backs that people may inexplicably drop.</p>"},{"title":"2022 Week 11","year":"2022","html":"<p>2022 Week 11: Thankful for THIS LEAGUE</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2b06\ufe0f ***Ch-ubb-y Chaser **Michael Costello</h2>\n<br>\n<p>No one likes it but <em>Costello</em> has put together a strong team thanks to a fleece of <em>Chorazyczewski</em> and another win-now type trade to sure up tight end. With his current roster makeup (two kickers, three QBs, and three defenses) he is not planning for the future. This year <em>Costello</em> is hitting on his long-term scheme and he is ALL IN.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2b06\ufe0f ***Derr-lick My Henry Balls** *Bradley White</h2>\n<br>\n<p><em>White</em> finds himself in familiar position as he moves back up in the Geraldo Index \u00a9. He\u2019s shored up his TE position with another win-now trade. His roster is fully healthy and capable of competing with, and beating, anyone. He has two top five RBs, a top tier QB, one of the top WRs in the game to go along with two other solid WRs. It\u2019s tough to see a world where he doesn\u2019t make the championship game.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2b07\ufe0f **Show Me Your TDs** *Braden Geraldo </h2>\n<br>\n<p>This team is like Turkey on Thanksgiving. No one really likes it but it wouldn\u2019t be Thanksgiving without Turkey. <em>Geraldo</em>, whether people like it or not, is always in championship contention. His team this year is no different, though it has more gaps than some of the other competitors as evident by last week, he still has a strong team equipped with the top three WRs in the game. He also has done it without making any win-now trades ensuring he will be competitive into the future.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2194\ufe0f ***Let\u2019s Cook** *Adam Banchiu* & *Matthew Steinmetz</h2>\n<br>\n<p>They won last year's championship but made a couple of questionable trades earlier this season that seemingly took them out of contention. HOWEVER. The redeem team is here! <em>Steinmetz</em> and <em>Banchiu</em> absolutely fleeced <em>Frink, securing</em> not only an affordable Mark Andrews but adding in a solid starting WR in Keenan Allen, which they desperately needed after trading away Waddle and Jefferson.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2b06\ufe0f ***The Mixon Administration ***James Laethem </h2>\n<br>\n<p>In what seemed like a lost season, <em>Laethem</em> is now the best of the rest. He made a big trade to put himself in position to compete this year, but gave up what looks like will be a decent first round pick. He has the best QB and the best TE in the game along with a top tier RB. WR is the big weakness, but Treylon Burks seems to be coming along. His team looks in good position to make a playoff run, but next year is likely to be better for him.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2b07\ufe0f* ***Me and Ma Homies** *Casey Smith</h2>\n<br>\n<p>In a pragmatic move, <em>Smith</em> dealt Mahomes to <em>Laethem</em> for a solid first rounder admitting his team isn\u2019t ready to be a real player this season. He is still in desperate need for some receiver help and a second reliable RB. This team should be better next year, but he doesn\u2019t seem to have a ton of strong keepers.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2194\ufe0f ***Patrick Bateman\u2019s **Blake Keaton</h2>\n<br>\n<p>This team is caught in the middle between winning now and building for the future. <em>Keaton</em> traded his first for an expensive Deebo Samuel in hopes that it would take him over the top\u2026 it didn\u2019t\u2026 He has some nice pieces for the future, but without a first rounder he shot himself in the foot at least for next year. At least he can sleep well knowing he took his best shot at winning the championship.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2b06\ufe0f ***Breece\u2019s Pierces** Jeffrey Butler</h2>\n<br>\n<p>This year keeps getting more and more wild as <em>Butler</em> has an outside shot (35% according to ESPN) at making it to the playoffs for the first time in league history. Not only did he expect to tank again this year, but he lost Breece Hall early on and didn\u2019t add a ton in the free agency draft with Samuel, Evans, and Zeke all having mediocre years. The five year plan might turn into a two year turn-around. But let\u2019s not forget it\u2019s FEJ.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2b07\ufe0f **Drunkin Polaks** Kyle Chorazyczewski</h2>\n<br>\n<p><em>Chorazyczewski</em> has made one questionable trade and another eh move for Michael Carter. He is in a weird position with only a couple of decent keepers, but he does have a 2024 draft pick so it might be a few years until he gets competitive again. Swift and Higgins aren\u2019t even starters on their own team and McCaffrey and Murray were still solid keepers to go along with his cheap receivers. We will never know why he did what he did, but we know he regrets it.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2194\ufe0f ***Smith-Schuster? Barely Know Her** *Collin Frink</h2>\n<br>\n<p>If things couldn\u2019t get any worse, <em>Frink</em> gave up his best keeper for a later first. He has decided to \u201cblow it up\u201d as they say in hopes to do a full reset. I don\u2019t blame him for trying since he was outkicking his coverage earlier in the year. His bad luck can be traced back to drafting CEH over Jonathan Taylor years ago a questionable move at the time that turned out to be terrible in hindsight.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2194\ufe0f ***Windy City Boys** *Campbell Gillespie</h2>\n<br>\n<p>There isn\u2019t much else to say about captain tank. He\u2019s a lock for at least the second pick in the draft and hoping for some luck for the first pick. He has another late first also so that is a good position for <em>Gillespie</em>. He has strong young keepers, but a history for panicking in the draft room so we will see if he can recover next year after a minor blip.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2194\ufe0f ***There\u2019s a YOU Sheriff in Town **Bobby Willen</h2>\n<br>\n<p><em>Willen</em> is in position to improve next year with two first round picks (one guaranteed to be in the top two). He has one of the top young backs in Etienne to go along with a few good WRs.  Expect big things out of this guy going into next season for the first time in franchise history. We shall see\u2026</p>"},{"title":"2022 Week 10","year":"2022","html":"<p>2022 Week 10:</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2194\ufe0f ***Show Me Your TDs** *Braden Geraldo </h2>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Past:</strong><strong> </strong>A+</p>\n<br>\n<p><em>Geraldo</em> leads the league history in most playoff appearances, only missing two in 11 years, and tied in first in number of titles with three. He has consistently competed for championships regardless of regular season records (only having the top record once).</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Present:</strong> A</p>\n<br>\n<p>With Cooper Kupp out for the foreseeable future, <em>Geraldo</em> has the three best receivers by a large margin. If Fields continues this steller run and Jones and Stevenson can continue to overcome their tough situations, <em>Geraldo</em> can go off any week. He\u2019s shown an elite upside in the White Power Rankings with three 11-0 weeks, two 10-1, and two 9-2 weeks.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Future:</strong> A</p>\n<br>\n<p>While he has no first round picks next year, he will be able to keep Fields for cheap along with the core of Jones, Stevenson, Diggs, and Hill. He has a few other potential keepers that could fill in some gaps along with the rights to Jefferson, who he could keep for an additional year. The short term future is bright, but the bubble will burst eventually</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2194\ufe0f ***Ch-ubb-y Chaser **Michael Costello</h2>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Past:</strong> C-</p>\n<br>\n<p>With only two winning seasons since taking the team over in 2013 <em>Costello</em> does not have a strong history. However, since he wrote the book on tanking in 2018, he has slowly build momentum and showed well last season (year 4 of his 5 year plan) by making the playoffs. Will he turn it around?</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Present:</strong> A-</p>\n<br>\n<p><em>Costello</em> appears to be a man of his word as he has put together a strong competitor in year 5 of his 5 year plan. This is the best chance in his history to win a championship and he is aware of that with aggressive \u201cwin-now\u201d moves to take his starting lineup over the top. His team has taken off in the White Power Rankings three of the past 4 weeks all since his big trade.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Future:</strong><strong> </strong>B+</p>\n<br>\n<p>He has no picks until 2025 and traded away the future rights to Higgins and Swift as well as Pitts on a rookie deal for players he may or may not keep. With that being said <em>Costello</em> has Ja\u2019Marr Chase</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2194\ufe0f ***Derr-lick My Henry Balls** *Bradley White</h2>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Past: </strong>A+</p>\n<br>\n<p>His resume is similar to <em>Geraldo</em>\u2019s except one fewer playoff appearance and one more division title. With the help of his 14-0 record last season he was able to take a one game lead in the overall regular season record. Three titles is the main reason for the grade and he\u2019s been a consistent winner dating back to 2011.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Present:</strong> A-</p>\n<br>\n<p>His team has taken a step back from last season, but <em>White</em> still has a strong chance to win his fourth title. Four of his top players from last year have taken step backs in terms of numbers either due to injury or new team situations. Godwin has been slowly coming back from a knee injury and along with Fournette the Bucs offense isn\u2019t as explosive as last year. Taylor and Adams have significantly worse teams this season impacting their performances. The Goedert injury hurts but he might be back by playoff time. <em>White</em> made a key move to get AJ Brown to pair with Hurts so he still has high upside any given week.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Future:</strong> B</p>\n<br>\n<p>He was able to fill the coffers with his genius preseason move of Waller for what mainly amounted to Pickens and Carter. Hurts is still cheap and Adams and Brown are relatively affordable with Henry being a steal at only $23 for the 2023 season. The issue comes beyond that with Jonathan Taylor becoming a restricted free agent, Godwin\u2019s price, and Fournette looking like he\u2019ll be out of the league next season. He will be competitive next year depending on which free agents he can secure, but this is the last year of his mini-dynasty.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2194\ufe0f ***Let\u2019s Cook** *Adam Banchiu* & *Matthew Steinmetz</h2>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Past:</strong> B+</p>\n<br>\n<p>The championship run last season off the backs of the most successful turn-around job in league history certainly cements <em>Banchiu</em> and <em>Steinmetz</em>\u2019s legacies. While</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Present:</strong> B+</p>\n<br>\n<p>Some aggressive moves complete changed the make-up of this borderline contender.</p>\n<br>\n<p>They traded away:</p>\n<br>\n<p>Justin Jefferson</p>\n<br>\n<p>Jaylen Waddle</p>\n<br>\n<p>Najee Harris</p>\n<br>\n<p>Jeff Wilson</p>\n<br>\n<p>They received back:</p>\n<br>\n<p>Saquon Barkley</p>\n<br>\n<p>Lamar Jackson</p>\n<br>\n<p>DK Metcalf</p>\n<br>\n<p>Diontae Johnson</p>\n<br>\n<p>Eagles D</p>\n<br>\n<p>Late 2023 First Round pick</p>\n<br>\n<p>Overall it improves their running game and gives them a strong QB they can trust. However they gave up two of the best young receivers in the game. Seems like a net neutral move in the present, but see below for the analysis on the future impacts. They are a near lock for the playoffs and should make it into the second round, but it will be a challenge to defend against the big 3.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Future:</strong> B</p>\n<br>\n<p>Here is where the trade gets interesting\u2026 It would be doubtful they can keep Barkley and Lamar, while great and young, is expensive for a QB. It would be ok if they had two starters for super cheap, but Waddle and Harris (both $9 in 2023) are no longer with them. DK and Diontae are good prices, but not extreme values. They do still have Ekeler ($29 in 2023) who is a plus value. The late first will help if they can hit on that and they can still retain Aiyuk as a restricted free agent so they have a solid future outlook.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2194\ufe0f ***Me and Ma Homies** *Casey Smith</h2>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Past:</strong> A-</p>\n<br>\n<p>Another multi-time champion, <em>Smith</em> has been a mainstay in the league since the start and is third all time in wins. He hasn\u2019t been as consistent at making the playoffs, but he is usually around .500 in his worst seasons. He is one of the top franchises and another championship run this season could cement him in the upper echelon of teams.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Present:</strong> B</p>\n<br>\n<p>His defense has been phenomenal and his offense has been consistent yet uninspiring. <em>Smith</em>\u2019s record doesn\u2019t reflect his championship hopes, but he has kept winning all year long despite never hitting 120 points. He has consistently been between 90 and 110, which means he isn\u2019t at risk to lose to bad teams or a team with an off week. Playoffs are a lock, but a playoff win is doubtful at best.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Future:</strong> B-</p>\n<br>\n<p>Kamara remains a strong keeper at a cheap price, but he is getting older. Mahomes is getting expensive for a QB but is still young and at the top of his game. Other than those two Lockett and Dulcich are the next best value keepers on his team. Unless Kareem Hunt ends up in a great position next season or <em>Smith</em> can get himself a good draft pick (unlikely given his current record) then he will need to rebuild through free agency,</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2b06\ufe0f** ***The Mixon Administration ***James Laethem </h2>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Past:</strong> B+</p>\n<br>\n<p>With two championships and a history of finishing making the playoffs, <em>Laethem</em> has been the top expansion team in league history. While his records don\u2019t always reflect that he has been clutch in key moments. Many say he won the break-up from <em>Chorazyczewski</em> in 2015 and it\u2019s tough to argue with that.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Present:</strong> C+</p>\n<br>\n<p>Injuries have been the story of <em>Laethem</em>\u2019s season. Losing Javonte made it difficult for him to compete for a championship, but he\u2019s done a great job managing the situation. Mixon and Kelce continue to be cornerstones for him, but he was smart to not make a \u201cwin-now\u201d move in the frenzy despite a decent chance at the playoffs. Wide receiver was going to be an issue for him entering the season and he wasn\u2019t able to address it much with waiver wire  players.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Future:</strong> A-</p>\n<br>\n<p>Waiver wire is the key to a successful roster building strategy and <em>Laethem</em> absolutely nailed it this year. He added OBJ and Ridley for next to nothing and while they won\u2019t help him this year they are going to be steals next year so long as they return to somewhat old form. With Javonte still on a cheap deal and Mixon relatively cheap he should be back in contention next year.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2b06\ufe0f ***Patrick Bateman\u2019s **Blake Keaton</h2>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Past:</strong> C</p>\n<br>\n<p>Despite making two championship games (and losing both) <em>Keaton</em> has a .500 record in the 11 years of the league. He is the epitome of average when it comes to the MSUFFL. He has some good players and always has, but hasn\u2019t ever made the leap to a champion. This year is going the route of .500 again.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Present:</strong> C+</p>\n<br>\n<p>His team is good this year with Josh Allen, Dalvin Cook, and Ceedee Lamb but it\u2019s missing that extra gear. <em>Keaton</em> has yet to really go off and none of his players is having a particularly great year. It might just be an off year, but there is still a chance for him to make a playoff push given his team and the division he is in. Going all in for Deebo is looking like a BIG mistake for <em>Keaton</em>.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Future:</strong> B</p>\n<br>\n<p><em>Keaton</em> still has Cook and Allen locked in with good value. Patterson and Singletary are good value but neither are that big of difference makers. Pacheco and Bateman could be the real deal, but they\u2019re not great at this point. He could use a first round pick but he traded it for Deebo who isn\u2019t great this year and isn\u2019t a good value.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2b07\ufe0f **Drunkin Polaks** Kyle Chorazyczewski</h2>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Past:</strong> B</p>\n<br>\n<p>His record since his split with <em>Laethem</em> he had a better record in the regular season but he\u2019s failed to seal the deal. Has <em>Chorazyczewski</em>\u2019s team been good? Yes. Does he miss his little buddy James? Yes. Get this guy some help!</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Present:</strong> C</p>\n<br>\n<p>He was on his way to another great season when he traded away two of his best players for two worse players. Adding in a pick was good, but <em>Chorazyczewski</em> killed his chances to be competitive this year since Swift and Higgins are a big step down from McCaffrey and Murray. He went from 4-2 with championship hopes to 5-5 in full tank mode.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Future:</strong> B-</p>\n<br>\n<p>Getting rid of Murray and McCaffrey hurts his future when you consider both Higgins and Swift are restricted free agents. <em>Chorazyczewski </em>also dropped Justin Fields for <em>checks notes</em> Zay Jones which looks like another boneheaded move. He has two first round picks which will help and Kupp is still cheap along with Marquise Brown. This franchise will have a lot of work to do to right the ship.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2b06\ufe0f ***Breece\u2019s Pierces** Jeffrey Butler</h2>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Past:</strong> F-</p>\n<br>\n<p>We all know <em>Butler</em>\u2019s story. He\u2019s won less than a third of games he\u2019s played in 11 years in the league. At his peak he was 6-7 just missing the playoffs. Last year he went full tank mode so maybe the restart will be good for him.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Present:</strong> C-</p>\n<br>\n<p>His team is actually looking competitive despite 1. Expecting to have a rebuilding year and 2. Losing Breece Hall for the year. <em>Butler</em> is moving up the standings and has an outside shot at making the playoffs for the first time ever. 7-7 is in his sights.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Future:</strong> A++</p>\n<br>\n<p>Breece Hall, Damien Pierce, Chris Olave, and Christian Watson are all showing out in their rookie years. Dotson looks solid and he can keep Mooney for cheap. He has three first round picks and two early seconds to add to his young nucleus. If he doesn\u2019t panic in the draft like he\u2019s known to do, see Zeke 2022 and LeGarrette Blount 2013, he might have a real shot at a championship and his first playoffs ever.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2b07\ufe0f* ***Smith-Schuster? Barely Know Her** *Collin Frink </h2>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Past:</strong> C-</p>\n<br>\n<p><em>Frink</em> has been up and down his whole career since going on his own from <em>Gillespie</em>. He\u2019s never been a championship contender and only made the playoffs twice. Needless to say he has yet to have success.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Present:</strong> D+</p>\n<br>\n<p>Everyone remembers the hot 3-0 start but that seems like ages ago at this point. He\u2019s fallen below <em>Butler</em> in his division without intentionally tanking. Losing Andrews for a few weeks definitely hurt him along with all the Chargers wide receiver injuries. Better luck next year kid.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Future:</strong> C</p>\n<br>\n<p>Andrews, Burrow, Smith, Mike Williams, and Jameson Williams are good keepers from a value or potential standpoint, but he still has no running backs. Sanders might be a decent keeper but he\u2019s not a top tier running back or a true value. <em>Frink</em> only has his first and second round picks but maybe he can make a move in the next couple weeks to change that.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2194\ufe0f ***Windy City Boys** *Campbell Gillespie </h2>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Past:</strong> B</p>\n<br>\n<p>He has had some success in the past, even winning a championship for a couple of days! <em>Gillespie </em>is considered a solid team historically but has had two waves of tanking. It\u2019s been a wild ride for this franchise.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Present:</strong> D</p>\n<br>\n<p>In full #blowforBijan mode, <em>Gillespie</em> isn\u2019t even starting his best players which is lame. He could still win some games with Waddle, St. Brown, Pittman, and Pollard but he\u2019s choosing to do what he knows best and tank.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Future:</strong> A</p>\n<br>\n<p>He\u2019s got a great young core of cheap keepers with Herbert, Walker, Waddle, St. Brown, and many more! He also has a chance at the first overall pick and has another late first rounder to work with. <em>Gillespie</em> was expecting to compete this year but he\u2019s built a strong team for years to come.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2194\ufe0f ***There\u2019s a YOU Sheriff in Town **Bobby Willen</h2>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Past:</strong> N/A</p>\n<br>\n<p>As the newbie in the league he doesn\u2019t have any history, but <em>Willen</em> will certainly leave a legacy going forward. It\u2019s yet to see whether it will be positive or negative.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Present:</strong> F</p>\n<br>\n<p>He was dealt a bad hand, but he\u2019s started to spend some of that giant FAAB budget to get some good players on his team. <em>Willen</em> is doing his best but still won\u2019t be competitive unless Etienne goes off, which he has been doing lately. Still his team STINKS.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Future:</strong> B</p>\n<br>\n<p><em>Willen</em> has a couple of solid pieces to build around and two picks to continue growing his team. I can see this team competing in the 2024/2025 time frame which is very impressive given the history of this franchises previous leadership.</p>"},{"title":"2022 Week 9","year":"2022","html":"<p>2022 Week 9: The Great Divide</p>\n<br>\n<p>After a week hiatus due to some league changing issues the season has accelerated towards championship push and #blowforbijan. There is a YOU Sheriff in Town leading the race to the bottom and a new leader in the Geraldo Index\u00a9 clubhouse.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2b06\ufe0f ***Show Me Your TDs** *Braden Geraldo </h2>\n<br>\n<p>The big move over the past couple weeks was a trade to secure 3 of the top 4 receivers in the league and gained a big advantage with the Justin Fields breakout. <em>Geraldo</em>\u2019s starting lineup is strong as long as David Njoku comes back to form soon and Fields continues his stellar run. RB is a little shaky, but they are both top 12 at the position with potential to go off.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2b06\ufe0f ***Ch-ubb-y Chaser **Michael Costello</h2>\n<br>\n<p>He made his big trade a couple of weeks back to secure three top 10 RBs (two top 5) and an effective QB along with Deandre Hopkins coming back and scoring a TD every week. <em>Costello</em> made a smaller trade to get George Kittle but gave up Kyle Pitts to signal that he is all in this year. If Jamaar Chase doesn\u2019t come back this season he is going to have a tougher chance winning, but if he does come back it\u2019s hard to see him losing to anyone.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2b07\ufe0f* ***Derr-lick My Henry Balls** *Bradley White </h2>\n<br>\n<p>Despite being a part of the \u201ctrade that broke the camel\u2019s back\u201d and acquiring AJ Brown for relatively cheap, <em>White</em> has fallen in the rankings for the first time all season. His team improved in theory but outside of Jalen Hurts and Derrick Henry his team has struggled to live up to their performances last season. Leonard Fournette is in danger of losing his job and Jonathan Taylor is struggling with a high ankle sprain. He proved last week he can beat anyone so he remains a championship contender.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2b07\ufe0f **Let\u2019s Cook** *Adam Banchiu* & *Matthew Steinmetz </h2>\n<br>\n<p>FINALLY they got a QB they can trust in Lamar Jackson but gave up Justin Jefferson to get him. They made another blockbuster trade which essentially came down to Najee Harris for Saquon and a late first round pick for Jaylen Waddle. It is unclear if they are looking to re-tool for a championship run or build for the future because one trade was giving up an elite talent for more keepable players and the other is a clear win now giving up two strong keepers on the second year of their rookie deal. They are just outside of the championship contender tier now.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2b06\ufe0f ***Me and Ma Homies** *Casey Smith</h2>\n<br>\n<p>I don\u2019t get how he is 7-2 and the current 1 seed if playoffs were to start today, but that\u2019s what happens when you\u2019re essentially playing the worst team in the league every week. Between Pat Mahomes and Alvin Kamara, <em>Smith</em> has two top players and Tyler Lockett has given him one reliable WR. He is a lock for the playoffs at this point, but can he secure the 1 seed? It\u2019s inevitable that <em>Smith</em> will get bounced from the playoffs unless the Irishman can keep his lucky charms.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2b07\ufe0f **Drunkin Polaks** Kyle Chorazyczewski</h2>\n<br>\n<p>There is a world where <em>Chorazyczewski</em> is 7-2 or 6-3 and waiting for Deshaun Watson and Marquise Brown to come back for the fantasy playoffs and lead him to his first championship. He gave that world away when he gifted an in-division opponent one of the best active fantasy RBs and a strong QB for a couple of chumps and a first round pick. He will be kicking himself for years for that deal and now finds himself in rebuilding mode maybe? Cooper Kupp isn\u2019t young, James Conner isn\u2019t elite, and Deandre Swift and Tee Higgins are restricted free agents next year, NOT IDEAL.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2b06\ufe0f *****The Mixon Administration *****James Laethem </h2>\n<br>\n<p>Joe Mixon is back and Travis Kelce never left! <em>Laethem</em> has a frisky team with an outside shot at the last playoff spot. He has solid starters in all other spots, but lacks the high upside (outside of another 5 TD game from Mixon) to beat the elite teams. He has a rough schedule down the stretch facing 3 of the top 4 teams over the next 4 weeks though so he might be prepping for next year early.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2b06\ufe0f ***Patrick Bateman\u2019s **Blake Keaton</h2>\n<br>\n<p>This guy has a solid team and is coming off of two strong performances, but his team just seems to choke in key moments (see the championship last season). This is another team that seems to be on the outside looking in, they have a big hill to climb to secure that last playoff spot. <em>Keaton</em> could feasibly make the playoffs but he too has a rough couple of weeks, at least he has Deebo Samuel and no first round pick!</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2b07\ufe0f **Smith-Schuster? Barely Know Her** *Collin Frink </h2>\n<br>\n<p><em>Frink</em> needs to win his division to secure a playoff spot, but it\u2019s looking unlikely due to a tough bye week this week. Mark Andrews coming back will certainly help next week and the rest of the season, but he will likely continue his tough stretch (1-5 since starting 3-0). It might be time to start the tank since he is closer to the bottom teams than the top teams from a record, talent, and mindset perspective.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2b06\ufe0f ***Breece\u2019s Pierces** Jeffrey Butler</h2>\n<br>\n<p>Nothing is more evident of a return to normal than <em>Butler</em> being back into the might make the playoffs but will likely won\u2019t category. His team is fighting hard despite losing his best player. It would be great for the Breece\u2019s Pierces culture to win a few games toward the end of the year and take that momentum into next season. <em>Butler</em> might make the playoffs next season, is the world ready for that?</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2b07\ufe0f **Windy City Boys** *Campbell Gillespie </h2>\n<br>\n<p><em>Gillespie</em> is addicted to the tank. He tanked in 2017, 2021, and now this year. Some say his addiction is why he gave <em>Geraldo</em> the championship in 2019 despite having the lead for almost a week! What was a promising team with Saquon Barkley turned into a nightmare and for that we are all sad\u2026 Not to rub it in, but <em>Gillespie</em> drafted Sky Moore over Garrett Wilson, George Pickens, and Damien Pierce just to name a few. It may or may not haunt him for years to come (it will).</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2194\ufe0f ***There\u2019s a YOU Sheriff in Town**** **Bobby Willen</h2>\n<br>\n<p>Here. We. Go. There is a new sheriff in town and he is YOU YOU YOU <em>Bobby Willen</em>. He has made 6 acquisitions since taking over the team formerly known as Blake Bortles Wikipedia Club. With Etienne in place and Hockenson in a new situation this team is starting to come alive. Bijan Robinson will add a nice piece to a team that is starting to shape up. I see <em>Willen</em> winning rookie of the year this season and making a potential playoff run next year. It would be a shame if he makes the playoffs before <em>Butler</em>.</p>"},{"title":"2022 Week 7","year":"2022","html":"<p>2022 Week 7 Spin Zone:</p>\n<br>\n<p>This is the part of the season where people start to tell themselves a story about how good their team is and why they have a shot to make a deep playoff run. Unfortunately the reality isn\u2019t always as shiny as they like to think.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2194\ufe0f ***Derr-lick My Henry Balls** *Bradley White </h2>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Spin zone</strong>:I haven\u2019t had all of my starters since week 1, none of them are out for the season, just some minor injuries and a couple of byes. My team is still stacked when available. I am still the best team in the league.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Reality:</strong> His team is not as good as it was last year. Adams, Taylor, and Fournette have been disappointing compared to last year and Godwins has been hurt. Depth is bad so any severe injury will be crushing. Hurts is the only thing truly working for <em>White</em> right now.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2194\ufe0f ***Show Me Your TDs** *Braden Geraldo </h2>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Spin zone:</strong> I\u2019ve scored the most points in the league. I have scored 120 points the last four weeks and five of the seven weeks. That\u2019s all with Lamar Jackson having a rough couple of weeks imagine when he goes back to 30 points a game. My team is dominant and I should be the one seed.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Reality:</strong> His running backs are ugly due to injuries and bad performances. Lamar Jackson is struggling and has been for weeks, now DK and Njoku are banged up and Sutton stinks. The high scoring games might be more luck than a reflection of reality.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2b06\ufe0f ***Ch-ubb-y Chaser **Michael Costello</h2>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Spin zone:</strong> I just traded for a stud QB and another stud RB. Now that Hopkins is back to being elite I have an unstoppable team. Pitts is going to figure it out and I will go undefeated the rest of the season all the way to a championship.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Reality:</strong> He\u2019s below 500 and that\u2019s with career best starts to the season for both Chubb and Jacobs. He is going to have to get really hot even through bye weeks and avoid injuries to make the playoffs. He literally only has two receivers.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2b07\ufe0f **Let\u2019s Cook** *Adam Banchiu* & *Matthew Steinmetz </h2>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Spin zone:</strong> We\u2019re 6-1 and the defending champs. We have one of the best receivers in the league to go with the best RB in the league. We\u2019re dominating despite a rough start for Russell Wilson, he\u2019ll figure it out eventually.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Reality:</strong> Outside of Ekeler and Jefferson they have a bunch of guys who can dud any week either due to bad offenses or not being the guy for their team. Harris and Montgomery are not explosive and are on terrible offenses and Waller is just as washed as Wilson. The luck is going to run out soon.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2194\ufe0f ***Drunkin Polaks** Kyle Chorazyczewski</h2>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Spin zone:</strong> I\u2019m winning my division and have good young talent. When I make the playoffs I will have Deshaun and Hollywood Brown back and I have Mike\u2019s pick. Man, I'm smart.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Reality:</strong> He traded two stud players to build for the future despite being one of the better teams in the league basically locked into the playoffs. <em>Chorazyczewski</em> might not make the playoffs to take advantage of Watson and Brown\u2019s returns. What was he thinking with that trade?</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2194\ufe0f ***Me and Ma Homies** *Casey Smith</h2>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Spin zone:</strong> I\u2019m winning the division and have the inside track to a bye. Defense wins championships and my team is dominating defensively right now (only two teams have scored more than 100 on me). I\u2019m like the 04 Stones.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Reality:</strong> He\u2019s been extremely lucky schedule wise to this point in the season. Looking at his roster <em>Smith</em> has two players (three if you count Carlson) that are locked in starters on most rosters and the rest are fringe guys. His best receivers are Christian Kirk and Tyler Lockett, come bye weeks he\u2019s in trouble.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2b06\ufe0f ***Windy City Boys** *Campbell Gillespie </h2>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Spin zone:</strong> My stockpile of second string running backs look to have all been promoted to at least timeshares or starters. Who said startups aren\u2019t great investments! My team is just starting to peak in time for a late season run.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Reality:</strong> He\u2019s 2-5 with a couple of tough bye weeks ahead so it will take a lot of luck to back into the playoffs. <em>Gillespie</em> still has a lot of players, outside of Barkley and now Walker, that are difficult to trust week to week that he needs to start. It might be too little too late.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2194\ufe0f ***Smith-Schuster? Barely Know Her** *Collin Frink </h2>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Spin zone:</strong> I\u2019m back on track after losing three straight. Good thing I started 3-0. Now my division is up for grabs and Burrow is getting hot at the right time. Gus Edwards and Keenan Allen are back healthy and I have three winnable games coming up.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Reality:</strong> He\u2019s only scored over 100 twice all season and hasn\u2019t done so since week 3. He has a huge question mark at RB2 and Mark Andrews and Mike Williams are now banged up. The pressure is on to win games.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2b07\ufe0f **The Mixon Administration** *James Laethem </h2>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Spin zone:</strong> I have the best TE in football, a stud RB, and one of the hottest QB throwing it. I\u2019ve been able to overcome some tough injuries and still remain competitive. My team is built different and the division is very winnable.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Reality:</strong> He\u2019s lost too much firepower to compete for a championship (and maybe a playoff spot). AJ Dillons terrible play on the field is an insult to the Javonte Williams injury. WR play was an expected weakness but the injuries haven\u2019t helped either. The only thing working is Kelce and he\u2019s on bye this week in a must win game.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2194\ufe0f ***Patrick Bateman\u2019s **Blake Keaton</h2>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Spin zone:</strong> I have the best QB in the game by far and Dalvin Cook is a perennial stud. I was able to get Deebo Samuel to boost my underperforming WRs, but with Dak back and CMC out of Carolina CeeDee Lamb and DJ Moore are going to be studs the rest of the year.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Reality:</strong> Despite Allen\u2019s dominance and Ertz outkicking his coverage, he has yet to score more than 110 in a week. That\u2019s hard to do with one player averaging almost 30. He is 3-4 and is far behind the league average in points for making his playoff hopes relying on an improbable late season winning streak.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2194\ufe0f ***Breece\u2019s Pierces** Jeffrey Butler</h2>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Spin zone:</strong> I beat Braden and Brad. I got Blake\u2019s first round pick and Kyle\u2019s second round pick. Breece Hall is an absolute stud and so are Olave and Pierce. My process is going according to plan!</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Reality:</strong> His process is going to plan, no one can argue with that. But let\u2019s not forget this is Jeff and something will inevitably go wrong when his team is actually competitive. Until then we wait to make fun of him for not making the playoffs.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2194\ufe0f ***Blake Bortles Wikipedia Club **Scott Simon</h2>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Spin zone:</strong> Etienne is the guy, Dak Prescott is back, and I\u2019m swimming in cash. Maybe I can make the playoffs interesting and win some games against better competition for some momentum into next year.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Reality:</strong> He looks at the app twice a week, once to set his lineup and again to see if he won. He doesn\u2019t understand the FAAB concept yet and refuses to completely rebuild. At least he doesn\u2019t complain like the previous manager!</p>"},{"title":"2022 Week 6","year":"2022","html":"<p>2022 Week 6 Trade Fever:</p>\n<br>\n<p>Trade Review: In an interesting development a 4-2 team decided to make what appears to be a future building trade with a 2-4 team looking to win now. I think this was generally fair but the clear winner is <em>Costello</em> for this season at least. He has two of the top five RBs and another top 10 along with one of the most talented young WRs and finally a stable QB. <em>Chorazyczewski</em> meanwhile definitely got worse until Deshaun Watson gets off the massage table, but Swift and Higgins are both solid starters with keeper potential. The first round pick shows <em>Chorazyczewski</em> is focused on the future.</p>\n<br>\n<p><em>Editors note</em> It\u2019s very clear by his reaction that <em>Steinmetz</em> wanted Murray bad and was salty after he was left out on the deal</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2194\ufe0f ***Derr-lick My Henry Balls** *Bradley White </h2>\n<br>\n<p>A week removed from a season high performance, <em>White</em> put up a huge dud with only 69.2 points. Bye weeks and injuries are going to test him more than a teething baby. Depth is a major issue, but his starting lineup is still as strong as ever when all his best are out there. Still <em>White</em> may want to look to trade future draft capital or one of his young players to add some depth.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Best trade partner:</strong> Breece\u2019s Pierces</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2194\ufe0f ***Show Me Your TDs** *Braden Geraldo </h2>\n<br>\n<p>Despite losing two starting RBs<em> Geraldo</em> continues to score week-in and week-out breaking 120 points for the third straight week to go with his 180 point explosion earlier in the year. He will eventually get back Elijah Mitchell to help with some additional RB and TE remains the major weakness on his roster. <em>Geraldo</em> may look to trade some of his depth pieces or future capital to improve his starting lineup. Don\u2019t be surprised if <em>Geraldo</em> tops the Geraldo Index\u00a9 next week.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Best trade partner:</strong> Windy City Boys</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2194\ufe0f ***Let\u2019s Cook** *Adam Banchiu* & *Matthew Steinmetz </h2>\n<br>\n<p>This is the hardest team to gage so far this season. Yes they are 5-1 and have firepower to beat any team any given week, but they also have the capability to flop. They got lucky this week with a win, but as bye weeks come <em>Banchiu </em>and <em>Steinmetz</em> might struggle to keep their one loss record (especially the next two weeks with Jefferson and Ekeler each missing a game). They definitely need a QB and probably also a stronger second RB. Look for them to package a couple of their younger players  for someone with more weekly upside.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Best trade partner:</strong> Chubby Chaser</p>\n<br>\n<p>Russell Wilson and Daniel Jones running into each other in the Let\u2019s Cook locker room\u2026</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2b06\ufe0f ***Ch-ubb-y Chaser **Michael Costello</h2>\n<br>\n<p>With an aggressive move <em>Costello</em> was able to deal for two top 5 players at their respective position without dealing his top 2 players instantly, making him a contender category despite a poor start to the season. He has basically  turned his team into a slightly worse version of <em>White</em>\u2019s team. If Kyle Pitts can live up to the hype and Deandre Hopkins has a late career resurgence look for <em>Costello</em> to get hot and potentially win the division. Needless to say if he doesn\u2019t win a championship this year we could be looking at this move as a hail</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Best trade partner:</strong> Smith Schuster? Barely Know Her</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2b07\ufe0f **Drunkin Polaks** Kyle Chorazyczewski</h2>\n<br>\n<p>On the other side of the trade <em>Chorazyczewski</em> might have made a panic move, but he got enough talent back where he can quickly reload while potentially still competing this season. QB is an obvious whole in his lineup but he still has Watson so as long as he makes the playoffs it shouldn\u2019t matter much, and saves him future headaches. Higgins adds some much needed receiver depth with Hollywood Brown out for the foreseeable future. Watch for this team in the playoffs if he makes it.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Best trade partner:</strong> Me and Ma Homies</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2b07\ufe0f **Me and Ma Homies** *Casey Smith</h2>\n<br>\n<p>Another team that has a bloated record, <em>Smith</em> really only has two consistent stars and a bunch of secondary pieces to pick from each week. He has some interesting pieces if he wants to be a win-now team, but he also has a decent core to build around. There will be a point in the season where he needs to decide to be a buyer or a seller because he will be a middle of the road team with his current roster. <em>Smith </em>will need another high-end skill weapon (either a WR or RB) and a trustable TE to live up to his current record.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Best trade partner:</strong> The Mixon Administration</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2b06\ufe0f ***The Mixon Administration** *James Laethem </h2>\n<br>\n<p>Week 6 was a character week for <em>Laethem</em>. He survived his first week without Javonte in Week 5 and managed to follow that up with a strong win over one of his rivals. Now with his team on a little win streak he looks to extend it against a team appears to be an equal match-up. If there is a move to be made it is either to add a better QB or add another elite skill player to pair with Mixon/Kelce.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Best trade partner:</strong> Chubby Chaser</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2b07\ufe0f **Smith-Schuster? Barely Know Her** *Collin Frink </h2>\n<br>\n<p>Another loss bringing his losing skid to three in a row, but <em>Frink</em> still remains a competitive team. His problem is he doesn\u2019t have a truly elite player outside of Mark Andrews but his team is solid all around. <em>Frink</em> remains a danger to every team in any given week, but that unfortunately means himself some weeks. He has some pieces to trade if he wants to buy or sell, so we will see which way he goes as we continue forward.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Best trade partner:</strong> Show Me Your TDs</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2b06\ufe0f* **Windy City Boys** *Campbell Gillespie </h2>\n<br>\n<p><em>Gillespie</em> was able to win an easy matchup, but more importantly he survived a bad performance from Herbert, a bye from one of his best players, and one of his players quitting or getting fired on their day off. Despite all of that, his team looks to be solid when everyone is healthy now that Kenneth (Ken? Kenny?) Walker is the man in Seattle. <em>Gillespie</em> should probably hold pat and maybe make a move for a more elite WR, but no one would blame him for moving some of his old expensive players for younger talent or draft picks because his team has a very strong core for next year and the foreseeable future.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Best trade partner:</strong> Breece\u2019s Pieces</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2b07\ufe0f* ***Patrick Bateman\u2019s **Blake Keaton</h2>\n<br>\n<p>Yes he has a 3-3 record, and yes he still has Josh Allen and Dalvin Cook, but losing Patterson and Bateman to injury has been tough on <em>Keaton</em>\u2019s starting lineup. They both should be back soon, but a brutal bye week in Week 7 is likely another L. If he makes the playoffs and some of his young WRs take the next step then he could make some noise, but I don\u2019t see him repeating a deep playoff run like last season. He really needs to trade for a reliable RB2 and another strong WR to pair with CeeDee Lamb.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Best trade partner:</strong> Derr-lick My Henry Balls</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2b07\ufe0f* ***Breece\u2019s Pierces** Jeffrey Butler</h2>\n<br>\n<p><em>Butler</em>\u2019s team still remains largely exciting for the future, he is still a few pieces away from frustrating the top teams consistently. Look for him to trade some of his expensive players to a WR needy team (Evans/Samuel) or someone looking for some stable RB depth (Harris/Moestert/Elliott). He will likely seek first round picks and/or young potential keepers to pair with his elite core of rookies. <em>Butler</em> will have a playoff team in no time!</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Best trade partners:</strong> Drunkin Polaks</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2194\ufe0f ***Blake Bortles Wikipedia Club **Scott Simon</h2>\n<br>\n<p>I am not sure what to say other than maybe he should start using some of that FAAB he saved up. He has $17 more than the next highest person and missed out on Jeff Wilson/Curtis Samuel who were relatively cheap every week starters and Deon Jackson/Kenyan Drake who are at least spot starters. Not sure what <em>Simon</em> is waiting for, but at this point he should be looking to both of his QBs and any player not under their rookie contract or a cheap keeper. Let\u2019s see what he does.</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Best trade partners:</strong> Anyone with picks!!!</p>"},{"title":"2022 Week 5","year":"2022","html":"<p>2022 Week 5 Check-In:</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2194\ufe0f ***Derr-lick My Henry Balls** *Bradley White </h2>\n<br>\n<p>Still at the top of the rankings after an amazing week 5, but looking ahead to this week is a good example of how shallow his depth is. With two starters out <em>White</em> is forced to start ancillary receivers and hoping to get some lucky bounces. If any of his guys does get hurt he will be dropping, but so far he\u2019s been lucky in the injury category.</p>\n<br>\n<p><em>Editors note</em> Davante Adams ABSOLUTELY ASSULTED a poor audio technician after the week 5 MNF game so a suspension is on the table which could cost <em>White</em>.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2b06\ufe0f ***Show Me Your TDs** *Braden Geraldo </h2>\n<br>\n<p>Another strong performance from <em>Geraldo</em> in week 5, but it\u2019s been a wild ride with injuries both in his favor and against him. It\u2019s clear that RB2 is a question every week, but his team is starting to stabilize with the emergence of Rhamondre Stevenson. Despite a couple of down performances from Lamar Jackson, <em>Geraldo</em> has broken the important 120 point mark 3 out of the 5 weeks.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2b07\ufe0f* ***Let\u2019s Cook** *Adam Banchiu* & *Matthew Steinmetz </h2>\n<br>\n<p>This duo didn\u2019t have a strong outing last week but got the W either way. <em>Banchiu</em> and <em>Steinmetz</em>\u2019s darling from last year Najee Harris is playing terribly as is Dangeruss Wilson, but Jefferson continues to carry the load. If/when Jeffrey Wilson returns to the bench their flex spot will become a question mark, so they will need to figure out how to fill that hole when they reach the playoffs.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2194\ufe0f ***Drunkin Polaks** Kyle Chorazyczewski</h2>\n<br>\n<p>Well well well\u2026 <em>Chorazyczewski</em> tops the <em>White</em> Power Rankings despite a 3-2 record and a team that appears to be on the decline. He still has two of the top players and Hollywood Brown looks legit in his new offense, but everything else isn\u2019t looking pretty. One injury and <em>Chorazyczewski</em> is leaning on JD Mckissic and Tevin Coleman (is he still in the league?), This team is in the Danger Zone to miss the playoffs with a few tough matchups over the next couple of weeks.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2b06\ufe0f ***Me and Ma Homies** *Casey Smith</h2>\n<br>\n<p>The big mover of the week! I\u2019m starting to believe a little bit despite all the lucky performances. <em>Smith</em> has a few solid players to go along with Mahomes and Kamara so the Geraldo Index\u00a9 is showing this team as an up and comer. This is a big week for for the Homies with a big matchup with <em>Banchiu</em> and<em> Steinmetz</em>. A win and a strong performance could mean another move up the rankings.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2b07\ufe0f* ***Ch-ubb-y Chaser **Michael Costello</h2>\n<br>\n<p>Guess the lights got a little too bright for <em>Costello</em> rising up the rankings only for his team to completely break down. A couple of key injuries have hurt him recently, but fortunately Brian Robinson has stepped into a starter-ish role. None of his injuries are long-term and Deandre Hopkins is about to come back and provide some much needed reinforcements at WR. After this week expect <em>Costello</em> to bounce back a little. The Geraldo Index\u00a9 does love the name change (take notes <em>Frink</em>).</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2b07\ufe0f* ***Smith-Schuster? Barely Know Her** *Collin Frink </h2>\n<br>\n<p>Now I'm free. Free fallin'. Yeah, I'm free. Free fallin'. Tom Petty might as well have been talking about <em>Frink</em> with his 1989 hit. A 3-0 start followed by two embarrassing losses shows that his team was indeed fraudulent. He is looking to get back on track this week, but has another tough matchup. Still <em>Frink</em>\u2019s team looks solid with upside to beat anyone any given week. The Geraldo Index\u00a9 can see him making the playoffs and that would be a win given his starting point.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2b06\ufe0f ***The Mixon Administration** *James Laethem </h2>\n<br>\n<p>Losing Javonte seemed like a death nail, but <em>Laethem</em> is showing why he is a championship level manager. He has solid depth across the board and guys that can go off at any point. Unfortunately he doesn\u2019t have the best talent, but he has enough to remain competitive. Don\u2019t sleep on <em>Laethem</em> this season, and according to his roommates from last weekend you literally can\u2019t!</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2b07\ufe0f* ***Patrick Bateman\u2019s **Blake Keaton</h2>\n<br>\n<p>Despite 35 from Josh Allen and 25 from Dalvin Cook his team still kind of stinks. <em>Keaton</em> has some decent looking players on paper, but the goal of the game is to score points and his players are just not good at that. The trap door is about to open on this team if they don\u2019t show out this week since they feel too highly ranked here. <em>BREAKING NEWS</em> while writing this <em>Keaton</em> is dropping below The Mixon Administration in the first ever live Geraldo Index \u00a9 adjustment.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2194\ufe0f ***Breece\u2019s Pierces** Jeffrey Butler</h2>\n<br>\n<p><em>Butler</em> dropped a winnable game last week due to some unlucky injuries and his starting lineup is starting to take form. Breece Hall and Damien Pierce are locked and loaded weekly starters to go along with a strong receiving core. QB and TE will hold him back from a wild playoff run but he will definitely be hurting others chances of making the playoffs late in the season.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2194\ufe0f* **Windy City Boys** *Campbell Gillespie </h2>\n<br>\n<p>He started strong, but it\u2019s been a tough few weeks for <em>Gillespie</em> as he was starting back up RBs and even a TE at flex. With Kenneth Walker stepping into a starting role there is a chance the Boys will start to look a lot better. There is upside here but playoffs are likely out of the question from this once storied franchise who won a championship for a couple days.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2194\ufe0f ***Blake Bortles Wikipedia Club **Scott Simon</h2>\n<br>\n<p><em>Simon</em> has the only team without a performance over 100 points, he also has had an open roster spot along with the most FAAB yet to be spent. People are wondering if <em>Simon</em> even has the ESPN app on his phone. Some of his draft picks are starting to look like real players so there is that, but he keeps winning games so that doesn\u2019t help his chances in the #blowforBijan race.</p>"},{"title":"2022 Week 4","year":"2022","html":"<p>2022 Week 4 Check-In:</p>\n<br>\n<p>What a season it\u2019s been so far! This is proving to be the most competitive MSUFFL Season we\u2019ve seen so far. Every week is chaos, and we are sure to have much more parody than we\u2019ve ever seen. Let\u2019s check in with each manager and see how they are feel!</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2194\ufe0f ***Derr-lick My Henry Balls** *Bradley White </h2>\n<br>\n<p>He finally lost a game and now Jonathan Taylor is out injured. <em>White</em> is still has some of the top players, but depth is going to be an issue if he loses any of his studs for long periods of time. Luckily the prognosis on Taylor is a week or two so <em>White</em> remains the championship favorite.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2b06\ufe0f ***Let\u2019s Cook** *Adam Banchiu* & *Matthew Steinmetz </h2>\n<br>\n<p>It pains me to see the Geraldo Index \u00a9 has this team in second this week, so consider this a wedding present for <em>Steinmetz</em> and an early birthday present for <em>Banchiu</em> (or a really really really early wedding present for him\u2026). They finally had a big blow-up week and showed that they can do it. Wilson seems to be getting more comfortable and Ekeler is finding his 2021 form. They are just behind <em>White</em> in the championship odds.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2b07\ufe0f* ***Show Me Your TDs** *Braden Geraldo </h2>\n<br>\n<p>Despite another big week of scoring, <em>Geraldo</em> dropped to 1-3 and has a tough journey ahead to make the playoffs. Though he leads the league in scoring it is a bit lopsided due to the huge week 2 performance, so more consistent 120 point outings will be needed to win enough games to back into the playoffs and make some noise. Needless to say <em>Geraldo</em> is not taking this season well.</p>\n<br>\n<br>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2194\ufe0f ***Drunkin Polaks** Kyle Chorazyczewski</h2>\n<br>\n<p><em>Chorazyczewski</em>\u2019s team has been the most consistent and has strong starters. However, his heavy reliance on the Cardinal\u2019s offense and lack of depth make his team shaky during bye weeks. Any injuries, especially to Kupp, McCaffrey, or Murray, will crash his chances to win his first championship. Until then <em>Chorazyczewski</em>\u2019s team will continue to be consistent.</p>\n<br>\n<br>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2b06\ufe0f ***Chasing Dimes **Michael Costello</h2>\n<br>\n<p>A big mover this week, <em>Costello</em> was able to overcome a key injury to Swift, that should hold him out at least another week. QB and TE remain to be a limiting factor of his upside, but WR and RB are so strong that he should continue to perform well. Handing <em>White</em> his first loss in the regular season since 2020 is a major confidence booster for <em>Costello</em>.</p>\n<br>\n<br>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2b07\ufe0f* ***Smith-Schuster? Barely Know Her** *Collin Frink </h2>\n<br>\n<p>Still a strong team that goes as far as Mark Andrews will take them, but they have shown some of the magic running out this past week. Sanders looks like the real deal, but Robinson and CEH appear to be heavily TD dependent. Allen will come back soon and Brady looks to be returning to his normal form as his weapons start getting healthy again. A loss this week could turn his carriage back into a pumpkin.</p>\n<br>\n<br>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2b06\ufe0f ***Me and Ma Homies** *Casey Smith </h2>\n<br>\n<p>Somehow <em>Smith</em> is 3-1 and staring 4-1 in the face despite being 8th in points for, seems like the luck of the Irish for this ginger. His team seems to be guaranteed to score between 90 and 110 every week since he has some stars but not a lot of lineup upside. His team has struggled with injury thus far so we could see <em>Smith</em> make it to the playoffs and make some noise, but it\u2019s unlikely he will win a championship.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2194\ufe0f ***Patrick Bateman\u2019s **Blake Keaton</h2>\n<br>\n<p><em>Keaton</em> doesn\u2019t really deserve to move up in the rankings, but due to a key injury that hurts <em>Laethem</em> and helps <em>Keaton</em> the Geraldo Index \u00a9 had to re-calculate. Javonte Williams being out for the year gives Melvin Gordon more season long upside (unless he fumbles the bag again). <em>Keaton</em>\u2019s team has broken 100 points once all season and has the third lowest points for all season, but left some big point totals on his bench frequently. <em>Keaton</em> is fringe (playoff) guy.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2b07\ufe0f** The Mixon Administration** *James Laethem </h2>\n<br>\n<p>A tough loss for <em>Laethem</em> losing one of his stud RBs, but he does actually have decent depth at the position. Unfortunately looking ahead to bye weeks and playoffs one of his strength is now gone and any further bumps in the road could create a disaster. <em>Laethem</em> still has a strong team but a weak start make playoffs seem like a long ways away which is why he is falling in the rankings.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2b06\ufe0f ***Breece\u2019s Pierces** Jeffrey Butler</h2>\n<br>\n<p>This has nothing to do with the fact that <em>Butler</em> knocked off the second rated team last week. It is the fact that he seems to have the top 2 rookie RBs and a top 3 WR in the class to go along with some strong vets. This is certainly the type of team who could cause some upsets throughout the season as evident by his week 2 performance.</p>\n<br>\n<br>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2b07\ufe0f **Windy City Boys** *Campbell Gillespie </h2>\n<br>\n<p>After securing his first win, you\u2019d think <em>Gillespie</em> would move up the rankings, but it was a bit lucky and his current roster looks a little depleted beyond the top couple of studs. With Zeke and Montgomery still in the mix it\u2019s difficult to see a path for him to have a starting caliber RB2. When bye weeks roll around he could be in serious trouble if he doesn\u2019t right the ship this next couple of weeks.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">\u2194\ufe0f ***Blake Bortles Wikipedia Club **Scott Simon</h2>\n<br>\n<p><em>Simon</em> who averages 4 wins a season through his career is 2-2 and is coming off back-to-back wins for the first time in his career (I assume). His points for are dead last and he doesn\u2019t have a single starting running back, but he moxie. He also is the only loss for two of the four 3-1 teams. This league!</p>"},{"title":"2022 Week 3","year":"2022","html":"<p>2022 Week 3 Quick Hitters:</p>\n<br>\n<p>NFL Team Comps</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-h2\">\u2194\ufe0f ***Derr-lick My Henry Balls** *Bradley White </h2>\n<br>\n<p class=\"wu-bold\">NFL Comparison:</p>\n<br>\n<p>If the Bills won on Sunday this would have been a more obvious comparison, but the Eagles match well to <em>White</em>\u2019s team since his team is looking dominant early in the season. He\u2019ll lose a couple of games here or there but without injury this team is going to be consistent and good.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-h2\">\u2194\ufe0f ***Show Me Your TDs** *Braden Geraldo </h2>\n<br>\n<p class=\"wu-bold\">NFL Comparison:</p>\n<br>\n<p>A team with high expectations entering 2022, <em>Geraldo</em>\u2019s team and the Bengals are similar in that they can be unstoppable when they\u2019re on but they\u2019ve been very inconsistent early in the season leading to a rocky early season record. They should still be a contender late into the season.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-h2\">\u2b06\ufe0f ***Let\u2019s Cook** *Adam Banchiu* & *Matthew Steinmetz </h2>\n<br>\n<p class=\"wu-bold\">NFL Comparison:</p>\n<br>\n<p>A championship favorite who started hot but fell to a bottom feeding team\u2026 sound familiar? They\u2019ll likely right the ship but they are certainly not bulletproof, both the Chiefs and <em>Banchiu/Steinmetz</em> have talent and a winning culture. Moving up in the rankings is more of who was in front of them last week than their performance (UGLY performance).</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-h2\">\u2b07\ufe0f **Drunkin Polaks** Kyle Chorazyczewski</h2>\n<br>\n<p class=\"wu-bold\">NFL Comparison:</p>\n<br>\n<p>They\u2019re good again, but not like they used to be. <em>Chorazyczewski</em>, much like the Packers, definitely still have MVP caliber players but aging and not performing to the high level we typically expect. They should make the playoffs and maybe even win their division even if his team isn\u2019t as good as the names on the team allude to.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-h2\">\u2b06\ufe0f ***Smith-Schuster? Barely Know Her** *Collin Frink </h2>\n<br>\n<p class=\"wu-bold\">NFL Comparison:</p>\n<br>\n<p>Are they good? I think they\u2019re actually good. The Jags got clowned for the Kirk signing much like <em>Frink</em> did on the Sanders signing, but it is definitely looking like a sharp move now. He has a solid team with just enough star power to beat anyone. This team is dangerous, but probably not a championship team yet. They need a new team name to be considered a top-tier team.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-h2\">\u2b06\ufe0f ***Chasing Dimes **Michael Costello</h2>\n<br>\n<p class=\"wu-bold\">NFL Comparison: </p>\n<br>\n<p>A great running game and solid receivers but a glaring gap at QB might cost them the playoffs or shot at a championship at the very least. They have a lot of young talent on the squad and always appear to be one piece away for that next level. Expect them to make some noise every week and if they can get things solved at QB they could definitely elevate to being a great team.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-h2\">\u2b07\ufe0f** The Mixon Administration** *James Laethem </h2>\n<br>\n<p class=\"wu-bold\">NFL Comparison:</p>\n<br>\n<p>They both have a lot of talent but are missing some key pieces at receiver right now. They\u2019ll figure it out and maybe even make the playoffs. <em>Laethem</em> will need their receiver core to come back at full strength and some players to step up for them to be a top tier team again. Until then the franchise cornerstones at TE and RB will have to outperform expectations to carry him to wins.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-h2\">\u2b06\ufe0f ***Patrick Bateman\u2019s **Blake Keaton</h2>\n<br>\n<p class=\"wu-bold\">NFL Comparison:</p>\n<br>\n<p>I\u2019m not surprised they\u2019re 2-1, but they\u2019re not as good as they were last year. They still are good and will definitely be in the playoffs at the end of the year, but their prime may be behind them. They have the talent to pop off, but it\u2019s weekly WR roulette to find who will lead the way. Brady kind of looks like <em>Keaton</em> now though so there is that\u2026</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-h2\">\u2b07\ufe0f **Windy City Boys** *Campbell Gillespie </h2>\n<br>\n<p class=\"wu-bold\">NFL Comparison:</p>\n<br>\n<p>The offense seems functional but defense is atrocious. They look better on paper than their record but still little hope to be a playoff contender. At 0-3 in a tough division the only hope is to catch fire and have a few lucky breaks. We've seen crazier things happen and <em>Gillespie</em> has the talent to do so.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-h2\">\u2194\ufe0f ***Me and Ma Homies** *Casey Smith </h2>\n<br>\n<p class=\"wu-bold\">NFL Comparison:</p>\n<br>\n<p>A surprise 2-1 team that everyone knows won\u2019t actually be good at the end of the season, but sometimes things bounce your way in a small sample size of games. <em>Smith</em> has a couple pieces of a good team, but too many gaps to be a top tier or even a second tier competitive team.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-h2\">\u2194\ufe0f ***Breece\u2019s Pierces** Jeffrey Butler</h2>\n<br>\n<p class=\"wu-bold\">NFL Comparison:</p>\n<br>\n<p>They have some real talent for the first time in a while. They won\u2019t win many games this year but should be feisty most weeks. <em>Butler</em> will definitely be better next year with one or two more players. Unfortunately they have a losing culture but they have a hard-nosed coach, er lawyer, in town to try and turn it around.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-h2\">\u2194\ufe0f ***Blake Bortles Wikipedia Club **Scott Simon</h2>\n<br>\n<p class=\"wu-bold\">NFL Comparison:</p>\n<br>\n<p>Wait, they won a game? How? Oh yeah I guess they have a few guys that can play. Hmm maybe they could be decent in a few years. I think <em>Simon</em> might be building something here, only time will tell. Maybe next year!</p>"},{"title":"2022 Week 2","year":"2022","html":"<p>2022 Week 2 Quick Hitters</p>\n<br>\n<p><em>Teams ranked based on Geraldo Index\u00a9</em></p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-h2\">Derr-lick My Henry Balls** *Bradley White</h2>\n<br>\n<p>This team only has one weakness and it\u2019s his WR depth. Curtis Samuel helps, but this isn\u2019t the bulletproof team it was last year. Still 2-0 and mostly healthy, <em>White</em> should cruise to a winning record and likely a first round bye.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-h2\">Show Me Your TDs** *Braden Geraldo</h2>\n<br>\n<p>After a tough week 1 where his team failed to score, <em>Geraldo</em>\u2019s team put together the highest scoring week in league history. The talent on his team is strong, but he will need to get a more reliable. A solid TE and RB2will help to maintain his performance.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-h2\">Drunkin Polaks** Kyle Chorazyczewski</h2>\n<br>\n<p>The Pols still have two of the top players in the league with McCaffrey and Kupp, who remains consistently dominate. However, outside of those two and Murray the rest of the team is going to be inconsistent relying on touchdowns to return value. Still <em>Chorazyczewski</em>\u2019s team is one of the best and has potential to beat any one any given Sunday.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-h2\">Let\u2019s Cook** *Adam Banchiu* & *Matthew Steinmetz</h2>\n<br>\n<p>They are one of three undefeated teams remaining, but the reigning champ has yet to put together a big outing. They are certainly capable, but QB is a big red flag and their RB trio is not performing like they did last year despite similar volume.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-h2\">The Mixon Administration** *James Laethem</h2>\n<br>\n<p><em>Laethem</em> has a very solid team with a high floor due to three strong RBs and the best TE in the game. Derek Carr and the WRs are however a problem if he wants to consistently win games. Unless Gabe Davis is a star or Burks gets it together this team won\u2019t make it deep into the playoffs, but should make it there at least.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-h2\">Chasing Dimes **Michael Costello</h2>\n<br>\n<p>A strong one-two punch at RB and a top 5 WR is the making of a strong core. There are some glaring weaknesses, like QB and depth at WR, but the big key for <em>Costello</em> will be getting top tier production from Pitts who has disappeared through two weeks.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-h2\">Smith-Schuster? Barely Know Her** *Collin Frink</h2>\n<br>\n<p>This was one of the harder teams to rank. On one hand he is 2-0, but on the other hand his roster is not sexy at all. <em>Frink </em>finally has three starting caliber RBs and he still can\u2019t break the top six in the Geraldo Index \u00a9 but I don\u2019t believe that Robinson, CEH, or Sanders are that special and it\u2019s going to be a weekly guessing game at WR and QB all season that will cost him games. Andrew\u2019s is the best part of his team.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-h2\">Windy City Boys** *Campbell Gillespie</h2>\n<br>\n<p>The points for and the record don\u2019t match up for <em>Gillespie</em>, but that\u2019s what happens when you face two of the top teams in the first two weeks. I expect a get right game against the \u201cHomies\u201d this week and if he gets some better health luck a bounce back in his record.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-h2\">Patrick Bateman\u2019s **Blake Keaton</h2>\n<br>\n<p>I know <em>Keaton</em> will be complaining about this ranking to everyone who will listen, but your team is just not that good buddy. Despite a fitting name change, some explosive, young WRs and the top QB, this team is not as strong as expected. Dalvin is looking like an afterthought in the new-look Vikings offense and it will be whack-a-mole trying to hit on WR each week leaving a lot of points on the bench.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-h2\">Me and Ma Homies** *Casey Smith</h2>\n<br>\n<p>Sneaking out a win in week 1 was fortunate for <em>Smith</em> as his team is not looking too strong to start the season. 100 points seems about right weekly with a lot of question marks outside of Mahomes and Kamara. People are starting to wonder if <em>Smith</em> is wasting their primes much like the lions did for Barry and Megatron.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-h2\">Breece\u2019s Pierces** Jeffrey Butler</h2>\n<br>\n<p>It\u2019s all going to plan, load up on some strong rookies and continue the tank to get some more. While <em>Butler </em>has put together some solid performances considering the make-up of his team, he is unlikely to compete this year and that\u2019s very evident through two weeks. Things should look better the later in the year we get.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-h2\">Blake Bortles Wikipedia Club **Scott Simon</h2>\n<br>\n<p>Two key players injured in each of the first two weeks definitely didn\u2019t help. Letting his wedding distract him from hitting the week 1 waiver wire to fill his lackluster roster also was a rookie move. If <em>Butler</em> has a plan then consider <em>Simon</em> the joker ushering in chaos.</p>\n<br>\n<p>2022 Week 1 Quick Hitters</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-h2\">Derr-lick My Henry Balls** *Bradley White</h2>\n<br>\n<p><em>White </em>is still a top team, but it might not be as easy of a ride as it was last year. He is on track to put up over 120 points, but benefited by a couple of 0 spots from <em>Gillespie</em> this week to have the inside track to the win.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-h2\">Show Me Your TDs** *Braden Geraldo</h2>\n<br>\n<p>Is it time to panic for <em>Geraldo</em>? Yes, yes it is. Only 1 touchdown from his skill players and an injury at RB already is not a great start for this once storied franchise.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-h2\">Me and Ma Homies** *Casey Smith</h2>\n<br>\n<p>Some good TD luck for <em>Smith</em> this week from Hunt, Mahomes, and Thomas. As predicted this team is very top heavy, but luckily the top of his team is really good.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-h2\">The Mixon Administration** *James Laethem</h2>\n<br>\n<p><em>Laethem</em>\u2019s team is looking dangerous with 3 solid RBs and Kelce still doing his thing. QB is the only question mark right now but that can be fixed pretty easily.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-h2\">Let\u2019s Cook** *Adam Banchiu* & *Matthew Steinmetz</h2>\n<br>\n<p>The defending champs didn\u2019t put up a great outing this week but should escape with the win. <em>Steinmetz</em> and <em>Banchiu</em> have what looks like the best WR in the league capable of putting up these numbers weekly meaning their team has potential to win every week.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-h2\">CeeDees DK **Blake Keaton</h2>\n<br>\n<p>Another unlucky touchdown day for <em>Keaton</em> but overall the team is looking solid. He had some questions answered with Patterson looking like he could be a weekly starter keeping the magic alive from last year.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-h2\">Drunkin Polaks** Kyle Chorazyczewski</h2>\n<br>\n<p><em>Chorazyczewski</em> had a strong output, on pace for 120s, and McCaffrey didn\u2019t get hurt. As long as his starters stay healthy he should continue to expect these types of outputs.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-h2\">Windy City Boys** *Campbell Gillespie</h2>\n<br>\n<p>Barkley is back in a big way and should lead to a strong season for <em>Gillespie</em>. Unfortunately week 1 is likely to be a loss and he has some holes in his roster he will need to figure out, but the arrow is pointing up.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-h2\">Smith-Schuster? Barely Know Her** *Collin Frink</h2>\n<br>\n<p>Does <em>Frink</em> have too many RBs? It looks like his prayers have finally been answered with CEH and Sanders showing out and Robinson recovering from injury in a big way. Now <em>Frink </em>needs to figure out his WRs, which looked to be the strength of his roster heading into the season.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-h2\">Chasing Dimes **Michael Costello</h2>\n<br>\n<p>This was a solid performance by <em>Costello</em> despite an injury to Higgins. His WR depth is non-existent but his RBs are very strong so he should be competitive week-in and week-out.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-h2\">Blake Bortles Wikipedia Club **Scott Simon</h2>\n<br>\n<p>Not much to say for <em>Simon</em> after this week. RB was an obvious weakness and nothing has changed after Etienne struggled and Mike Davis was mostly a no-show. This is looking like the first overall pick team as predicted.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-h2\">Breece\u2019s Pierces** Jeffrey Butler</h2>\n<br>\n<p>Still in rebuild mode, but <em>Butler</em> has a chance to win this week and be competitive going forward. His young guys had some ups and downs so expect his team to improve as the season progresses. He could make some noise, but I bet he wishes he paid up for Barkley!</p>"},{"title":"2022 Pre-Season","year":"2022","html":"<p>2022 Pre-Season Report</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">Elite:</h2>\n<br>\n<p>Winners of three championships each, it\u2019s more of a surprise if they miss the playoffs than win their division. These two dynasties are the best of the best.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-h2\">Derr-lick My Henry Balls** *Bradley White</h2>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Team Grade:</strong> A+ (Win now)</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Expected Outcome:</strong> Championship Contender</p>\n<br>\n<p><em>White</em> has again put together a strong team. He was able to keep the majority of his core, only cutting Dak and trading Waller (both moves he could afford due to depth). He didn\u2019t get much at the draft\u2026 likely because he was distracted by Peyton\u2026 but he did make a major move over wedding cocktails with Banchiu and Steinmetz. <em>White</em> moved an aging tight end for a borderline starting QB and two young players in Carter and Pickens. This was definitely a plus move as his team was getting very very old. Either way <em>White</em> will be a playoff team and he will compete for his fourth \u2018ship.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-h2\">Show Me Your TDs** *Braden Geraldo</h2>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Team Grade:</strong> A (win now with some solid youth)</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Expected Outcome: </strong>Championship Contender</p>\n<br>\n<p>Strong keepers and a stronger draft leads to another championship caliber team. <em>Geraldo</em> was four deep and WR and RB and locked up Lamar to a solid deal to lead the charge. In the draft he was able to secure another solid RB with upside and two more cheap WRs. No surprises here as <em>Geraldo</em> pushes in the #racetofour against his arch nemesis<em> </em>White*.</p>\n<br>\n<p><em>*editor's note: this is a Michigan/Ohio State situation where Geraldo never actually beats White but still considers them as rivals.</em></p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">Winners:</h2>\n<br>\n<p>Winner of one or two championships and they win more than they lose over the years. These three franchises typically make the playoffs and contend for division titles year after year. Some are on the rise (<em>Banchiu</em> and <em>Steinmetz</em>) others are one the decline (<em>Smith</em>).</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-h2\">Me and Ma Homies** *Casey Smith</h2>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Team Grade:</strong> B- (should rebuild but won\u2019t)</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Expected Outcome:</strong> Borderline playoff team</p>\n<br>\n<p>Another year, another putrid offseason for <em>Smith</em>. Coming off a sub-par year for his standards he had a glaring need for WR (despite spending all his capital last season at the position). <em>Smith</em> elected not to keep Thomas or Mclaurin to maximize resources to address the need. He did get a solid deal on Thomas (if he comes back healthy finally), but spent considerable resources on OLD OLD OLD receivers. If you squint, you can see a competitive team here but you definitely have to paint a picture.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-h2\">The Mixon Administration** *James Laethem</h2>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Team Grade:</strong> B (win now)</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Expected Outcome:</strong> Probable playoff team</p>\n<br>\n<p><em>Laethem</em> has a solid core with Javante, Mixon, and Kelce. He added Burks via the rookie draft, Gabe Davis/Allen Lazard, two WRs with high potential, in free agency, and don\u2019t forget about the AJ Dillon trade (which some mocked at the time, not me, but some) which is looking better and better by the day. Is <em>Laethem</em> back? Maybe! Probably not, but maybe! He should be competitive and contend for the playoffs this year.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-h2\">Let\u2019s Cook** *Adam Banchiu* & *Matthew Steinmetz</h2>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Team Grade:</strong> A (win now with solid youth)</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Expected Outcome: </strong>Championship Contender</p>\n<br>\n<p>The defending champs kept their squad largely intact by keeping RFA David Montgomery along with the rest of their core but let Kittle walk in free agency. <em>Banchiu </em>and <em>Steinmetz</em> did get aggressive to replace Kittle with Waller by giving up a first (Pickens) and Michael Carter along with Aaron Rodgers. It definitely makes their lineup better on paper, but they gave up a lot for a player that may be on the decline. Still having two top RBs and the best young WR in the game.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">Can\u2019t Close:</h2>\n<br>\n<p>These three franchises usually make the playoffs and sometimes win a division if things break their way. They have the potential to upset one of the stronger teams every year, but just can\u2019t get it done in the playoffs. One of them (Gillespie) felt the feeling of a winner for a couple days, but couldn\u2019t overcome the odds.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-h2\">CeeDees DK **Blake Keaton</h2>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Team Grade: </strong>B (win now)</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Expected Outcome: </strong>Probable playoff team</p>\n<br>\n<p>Coming off his second, second place finish <em>Keaton</em>\u2019s team looks much worse on paper than at the end of last season. Allen and Cook are locks to be the top of their positions and Moore and Lamb are going to be weekly locks in his lineup, but beyond that YIKES. His draft was rough to say the least. His big signings were two 30 year old RBs in Melvin Gordon and Cordarrell Patterson\u2026 hmm. Garrett Wilson has potential to be a future starter but the Jets ecosystem stinks. He\u2019s in a tough division so we will see but he is likely to make the playoffs still.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-h2\">Drunkin Polaks** Kyle Chorazyczewski</h2>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Team Grade:</strong> A (win now with solid youth)</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Expected Outcome: </strong>Championship contender</p>\n<br>\n<p><em>Spoiler alert</em> <em>Chorazyczewski</em> is the last of our Championship contenders this year. He has a solid roster with a mix of young up and comers and solid vets. No one is saying that <em>Chorazyczewski</em> set up the Brian Robinson shooting, but no one is saying he didn\u2019t (there is clearly a motive\u2026 just saying). As long as McCaffrey and Connor can stay healthy this Polak squad is a candidate to lead the week in scoring every week. When we asked for his comments on the Watson signing, <em>Chorazyczewski</em> said \u201cI\u2019m not a pervert.\u201d All in all this was a solid offseason for the <em>Drunkin Polaks</em>.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-h2\">Windy City Boys** *Campbell Gillespie</h2>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Grade: </strong>B+ (win now or blow it up, no in between)</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Expected Outcome: </strong>Playoff contender</p>\n<br>\n<p>The rookie draft didn\u2019t go his way with <em>checks notes</em> Sky Moore and Zamir White? You never know with rookies but he could have had James Cook, Garrett Wilson, or Damien Pierce who are all in line to get immediate work. In his defense he did lock in Kenneth Walker who if he gets healthy will likely be a strong player for years to come. <em>Gillespie</em>\u2019s free agency went much better than the rookie draft locking in two of the best players at positions of need in Saquon Barkley and George Kittle. His team has a few question marks, but all in all it\u2019s going to be a decent season for <em>Gillespie</em>.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">Punching Bags:</h2>\n<br>\n<p>Some might say these guys are the lifeblood of the league. Others might say they stink. Either way they pay their dues on time and take their Ls with class, usually. They struggle to reach 500 and any playoff birth is a bonus, but there is always hope!</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-h2\">Smith-Schuster? Barely Know Her** *Collin Frink</h2>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Grade: </strong>B- (should blow it up but won\u2019t)</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Expected Outcome: </strong>First overall pick contender</p>\n<br>\n<p>In his defense it was his wedding week, but <em>Frink</em> failed to address the running back position for the ninth offseason in a row. $36 on Miles Sanders! Miles Sanders\u2026 this guy is addicted to RB 2s. I want to take it easy on him so I won\u2019t mention CEH over JT. I will say he has some depth at QB and WR he can use to get stronger at RB either this year or in the future so I wouldn\u2019t be surprised if <em>Frink</em> got aggressive in the trade markets early this season. If not, expect him to be back in this same position next year.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-h2\">Chasing Dimes **Michael Costello</h2>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Grade:</strong> A- (win now with some solid youth)</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Expected Outcome:</strong> Probable playoff team</p>\n<br>\n<p>We\u2019re entering year 4 of <em>Costello</em>\u2019s 5 year plan and it\u2019s actually starting to look realistic that he could become a contender over the next couple of years. Some could argue he had the best draft out of anyone. He cut the fat off his roster and loaded up on some of the top available talent while keeping his RFA Josh Jacobs for relatively cheap. Where it gets dicey is at QB, but he has options to hold him through until he can make a trade or luck into a strong season. Chase is likely to be a stud again along with the solid RB combo of Chubb and Swift. This probably isn\u2019t a championship team yet but it definitely could get there if things break the right way. I anticipate this franchise moving up a tier next season.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-h2\">Blake Bortles Wikipedia Club **Scott Simon</h2>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Grade: </strong>C+ (rebuilding)</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Expected Outcome: </strong>lock for top 3 pick</p>\n<br>\n<p>As we get older it\u2019s easy to find solace in things that do not change. <em>Simon</em>\u2019s ability to continuously rebuild is one of those comforting traditions. He had a solid draft getting the top WR in the draft by many accounts and two RBs with tremendous upside. He also kept RFAs Hockenson and AJ Brown while adding stud young WR Terry Mclaurin. Don\u2019t get me wrong <em>Simon</em> will win some games, but just enough to cost himself the top pick again. Honestly though if Etienne hits he could have a solid team in the next couple of years. Better luck next year Scotty!</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-team-header\">Playoffs?:</h2>\n<br>\n<p>This franchise finally played more than 13 games this past year! Unfortunately it\u2019s only due to the NFL expanding from 17 to 18 games. It\u2019s shocking that one of the original five franchises has failed to win more games than he\u2019s lost in every season.</p>\n<br>\n<h2 class=\"wu-h2\">Breece\u2019s Pierces** Jeffrey Butler</h2>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Grade: </strong>Incomplete (rebuilding)</p>\n<br>\n<p><strong>Expected Outcome: </strong>guaranteed to miss the playoffs</p>\n<br>\n<p>Little did Jim Mora know when he gave his infamous rant in 2001 that he would be referenced every single MSUFFL write up until the end of time, but <em>Butler</em> has made it possible and he deserves some of the royalties. The crazy part is, <em>Butler</em>\u2019s roster isn\u2019t half bad despite playing the long rebuild game. He\u2019s got two decent WRs and a few young guys with potential. If Breece Hall hits <em>Butler</em> might be back to his 5-9/6-8 records we\u2019ve taken for granted. The free agency did not go his way however. We\u2019ve all been there, a stack of cash with so many attractive options when all the sudden the first decent option makes themself available and boom half your money is gone and you don\u2019t even know what happened. This is looking like a solid long-term rebuilding team though.</p>"}];
